A ternary plot needs values on the nonnegative unit simplex in 3D, so normalize the 3 components and map them with an affine transform to 2D. For your function:

ClearAll[f1];
f1[x_] := {Abs[Sin[x]], Mod[x, 2], Abs[Cos[x]]};

With[{xyToTernary = {{0, 1, 1/2}, {0, 0, Sqrt[3]/2}}},
 ParametricPlot[
  xyToTernary . (f1[x]/Total[f1[x]]), {x, 1, 3^5},
  Exclusions -> Range[3^5],
  AxesOrigin -> {0, 0}, PlotRange -> {0, 1},
  Prolog -> {White, EdgeForm[Black], Polygon[{{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1/2, Sqrt[3]/2}}]}
 ]
]

If you want Mathematica to handle the 3D-to-2D projection directly, use ParametricPlot3D with a simplex and view it along {1,1,1}.

You can group the combined lists by the shared first 4 elements, then merge the tail parts.

For example, with your data:

list1 = {{a,b,c,{d,e,f},10,21},{m,n,p,{r,s,t},14,64}};
list2 = {{m,n,p,{r,s,t},12},{a,b,c,{d,e,f},16}};

you can use:

Gather[list1 ~Join~ list2, #1[[;;4]] == #2[[;;4]] &] /. 
  {{{h__, m_List, t1__}, {h__, m_List, t2__}} :> {h, m, t1, t2},
   {l : {__, _List, __}} :> l}

This gives:

{{a,b,c,{d,e,f},10,21,16},{m,n,p,{r,s,t},14,64,12}}

It’s suited to your case where the matching part is always in the same position.

You can use the same pattern and switch the lattice type to simple cubic:

basis = LatticeData["SimpleCubic", "Basis"];
points = Tuples[Range[-4, 4], 3].basis;
inside = Select[points, Norm[#] <= 4 &];
Graphics3D[Point[inside]]

If you want a 3D simple cubic lattice extending 4 units in each coordinate direction, this generates the lattice points and then keeps the ones within radius 4. If you mean a 4×4×4 grid, use Range[0, 3] instead of Range[-4, 4].

You can map each lattice value to an arrow graphic and place those graphics in a grid. In Mathematica/Wolfram Language, something like this works:

angles = {{0, π, π}, {0, 0, π/2}, {π/2, 0, 3 π/3}};

GraphicsGrid[
  Map[
    Graphics[{
      LightGray, Circle[{0, 0}, 1],
      Hue[#/(2 π), .6, .8], Thick, Arrowheads[Medium],
      Arrow[{{0, 0}, {Cos[#], Sin[#]}}]
    }] &,
    angles, {2}
  ]
]

This makes a 3×3 lattice of circle-and-arrow glyphs, one per matrix entry. If you want, you can make the plot fancier by adjusting the Graphics styling.

If you want the likeliest path, the closest supported approach is to use the process’s transition matrix and create a new discrete Markov process with the desired starting state. The evidence shows the initial state can be changed this way:

mp = DiscreteMarkovProcess[1, transitionMatrix];
mp = DiscreteMarkovProcess[2, MarkovProcessProperties[mp, "TransitionMatrix"]];

It also suggests that directly changing the first part, like mp[[1]] = 2, works in that example. If by “likeliest path” you mean the most probable sequence of states, that specific calculation isn’t covered here.

Yes. If your Graphics3D is built from Polygon objects, you can extract the polygon vertices and use those as surface points. The approach shown is:

p1 = addHatToL /@ Take[Flatten[Take[LsOnDodecahedron, All]], All];
surfacepoints = Flatten[
  Flatten[Cases[Flatten[p1[[#]]], Polygon[m_] -> m], 1] & /@ Range[Length[p1]],
  1
];

This returns the points on the surface of the object. It does not directly test whether arbitrary 3D points are inside the object, but it gives you the surface point table you asked for.

Yes—this can be done by monitoring the active tab and resetting the tab’s local variable when that tab is selected.

A working pattern is to keep the variable inside a `DynamicModule`, and use an invisible `Dynamic` that runs `x = False` whenever the tab changes:

```wl
DynamicModule[{tab = 1},
 TabView[{
   DynamicModule[{x = False},
    Row[{
      Dynamic[tab; x = False; "", TrackedSymbols :> {tab}],
      {Checkbox[Dynamic[x]], Dynamic[x]}
    }]],
   "foo"
  },
  Dynamic[tab]
 ]
]
```

The key idea is that `x` stays local to the tab, and the tab selection triggers a reset.

Use Read with a token pattern that includes the commas as separators, then remove the commas from the result:

stream = StringToStream["Apple,Jack,1,123.456\nOrange,Jill,2,456.789\n"];

While[! EndOfFile === (data = 
    Read[stream, Riffle[{Word, Word, Number, Number}, Word],
      TokenWords -> {","}] /. {"," -> Sequence[]}),
  Print["Fruit:", data[[1]], " Name:", data[[2]],
    " Integer:", data[[3]], " Real:", data[[4]]];
];

Close[stream];

This works without converting the stream.

Use a nested pure function so the two #’s don’t collide. For example:

Select[{1, 2, 3, 4, 5},
  Function[i, IntervalMemberQ[Interval[#], i]]
] & /@ {{0, 1}, {1, 4}, {3, 4}}

This gives:

{{1}, {1, 2, 3, 4}, {3, 4}}

The inner Function uses # as the interval bounds, and i is the element being tested. If you want to exclude a single controlled element, use the same idea: make that element a separate argument rather than reusing # for both roles.

You can extract the line data from the Plot and export it as a table:

data = Cases[Plot[Sin[x], {x, 0, 2 Pi}], Line[data_] :> data, -4, 1][[1]];
Export["file.txt", data, "Table"]

If you want the points used during plotting, use EvaluationMonitor:

data = Reap[
  Plot[Sin[x], {x, 0, 2 Pi}, EvaluationMonitor :> Sow[{x, Sin[x]}]]
][[2, 1]];
Export["file.txt", data, "Table"]

For multi-line plots, extract all Line data with Cases[First@gr, Line[data_] :> data, -4] and export each list separately. If you don’t need Plot’s adaptive sampling, Table[{x, Sin[x]}, ...] is the simplest way to generate x-y data directly.

If you already have the plot, you can extract the sampled curve data from its graphics primitives:

data = Cases[Plot[Sin@x, {x, 0, 2 Pi}], Line[data_] :> data, -4, 1][[1]];

Then export it as two columns:

Export["file.txt", data, "Table"]

For a multi-line plot, extract all Line primitives from the graphics:

gr = Plot[{Sin@x, Cos@x, Sinc@x}, {x, 0, 2 Pi}];
multidat = Cases[First @ gr, Line[data_] :> data, -4];

Then export each dataset separately if needed. If you don’t need adaptive sampling, you can generate the points directly with Table[{x, Sin@x}, {x, 0, 2 Pi, 0.01}].

You can combine two ArrayPlots by multiplying them, which blends the colors into new shades:

ImageMultiply @@ Table[
  ArrayPlot[RandomReal[{0, 1}, {5, 5}],
    ColorFunction -> (Blend[{White, color}, #] &)],
  {color, {Yellow, RGBColor[0, 0.6, 1]}}
]

If you want to do it at the raster level, use the underlying raster data and keep the graphics options:

gr = Table[
  ArrayPlot[RandomReal[{0, 1}, {5, 5}],
    ColorFunction -> (Blend[{White, color}, #] &)],
  {color, {Yellow, RGBColor[0, 0.6, 1]}}
];

Graphics[
  Raster[gr[[1, 1, 1]]*gr[[2, 1, 1]]],
  Options[gr[[1]]]
]

This supports combining two plots into a single plot with mixed colors.

Use the relation Q(x) = Erfc(x/Sqrt[2])/2. In Mathematica, define it as

q[x_] := Erfc[x/Sqrt[2]]/2;

If you want it to be available every time you open the notebook, put the definition in an initialization cell and set it to run on startup.

`Eigensystem` returns eigenvalues in the order most convenient for the underlying algorithm, not in numeric order. For symmetric/Hermitian cases, that can still be by absolute value. If you want a different order, sort them yourself and reorder the eigenvectors the same way:

```mathematica
{ε, ψ} = Eigensystem[a];
{ε, ψ} = {ε[[#]], ψ[[#]]} &@Ordering[ε];
```

This is especially relevant because for non-Hermitian matrices the eigenvalues may be complex, and there is no natural built-in ordering for complex numbers.

You can read it in chunks as strings, then convert each chunk from CSV text into a table. For example:

Clear[readRows];
readRows[stream_, n_] :=
  With[{str = ReadList[stream, "String", n]},
    ImportString[StringJoin[Riffle[str, "\n"]], "Table"] /; str =!= {}];
readRows[__] := $Failed;

str = OpenRead["file.csv"];
While[readRows[str, 1000] =!= $Failed, (* process each batch *)];
Close[str];

This supports streaming large CSV files. The provided material does not show a specific “remove comments” step, so if your comments are lines you want to skip, the closest guidance here is to read the file as strings and filter out those lines before importing them.

Use Blend directly inside the graphics, mapping your distance value through anchor points:

Show[
  Function[attributes,
    Graphics[{
      Blend[{{-Max[Abs[distance]], Red}, {0, LightRed}, {0, LightGreen}, {+Max[Abs[distance]], Green}},
        distance[[attributes]]
      ],
      Rectangle[
        {If[distance[[attributes]] < 0, distance[[attributes]]*10, 0], attributes - 1},
        {If[distance[[attributes]] < 0, 0, distance[[attributes]]*10], attributes}
      ]
    },
    PlotRange -> {{-5, 5}, {0, 10}},
    Epilog -> {White, Line[{{0, 0}, {0, 11}}]}
    ]
  ] /@ Range[10],
  Frame -> True
]

This makes negative values shade toward red and positive values toward green.

To avoid breaks, don’t rely on a single automatic Plot3D grid. The supported approach is to build a custom grid that is dense near the important features and coarse elsewhere.

The key idea is:
- identify the boundary where the function reaches a chosen cutoff, and the ridge/minimum line you want to emphasize;
- switch to polar coordinates centered at the data’s center;
- use interpolation to trace those lines;
- generate a tailored sampling grid for the plot.

This improves resolution near max/min regions and removes the appearance of gaps. In the shown solution, PlotRange is also cut below 2 so the low-value region is excluded.

No. The issue described is not that Mathematica’s matrix multiplication or inverse is “wrong,” but that the symbolic matrix was being treated as singular because of numerics/very small entries. The answer says this can happen when tiny values get chopped to zero or when Mathematica can’t determine invertibility symbolically.

The suggested fix is to rationalize the matrix elements before inverting:

c = Rationalize[Cttt, 10^(-50)];
e = Rationalize[E0, 10^(-50)];
k = Inverse[c.e.Transpose[c]];

So the closest supported guidance is: use better numeric handling or rationalization; with a numeric parameter value the inverse works, and the determinant checks out.

The issue is that Rasterize can make tick marks too small when ImageResolution increases, because the graphics are scaled down after tick sizes are set.

A workaround is to export the plot to PDF and import it back before rasterizing:

plot = Plot[x, {x, 0, 10}];
h = First@ImportString[ExportString[plot, "PDF"]];
Rasterize[h, ImageResolution -> 300]

This keeps the ticks at the same relative size as in the notebook and also gives you an intermediate vector-style graphic that can be resized more consistently.

The provided example shows using a `Button` with `Manipulate` by setting a boolean control variable and then exporting when that variable becomes `True`. It does not specifically address fraction alignment in a JPG export, but it does support the general pattern of triggering `Export` from a button.

A close approach is:

filePath = FileNameJoin[{HomeDirectory[], "DeskTop", "test.jpg"}];
Manipulate[
  p = Plot[Sin[x - n], {x, 0, 4 Pi}, ImageSize -> 300];
  If[export, Export[filePath, p]; export = False];
  p,
  {n, 0, 2 Pi, Appearance -> "Labeled"},
  {{export, False}, None},
  Button["Export", export = True]
]

If you mean something else by “fraction alignment,” this evidence doesn’t cover it.

If you can solve the polynomial for Z after substituting a[x], b[x], c[x], then expand each solution as a series in x and extract coefficients.

For example:
sol = Z /. Solve[F[Z, a[x], b[x], c[x]] == 0, Z];
sol2 = Series[sol, {x, x0, n}];
coeff = Table[Coefficient[sol2, x, i], {i, 1, n}];

If there are multiple roots, coeff will contain the coefficients for each branch. If the expression is too complicated, do the Series first and then take coefficients. To get the truncated polynomial, use Normal[sol2].

To transform an image like a primitive, convert it to a texture mapped onto a polygon. The example uses:

t = Texture@ExampleData[{"TestImage", "Lena"}];
p = {t, Polygon[{{0, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 1}, {1, 0}}, 
  VertexTextureCoordinates -> {{0, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 1}, {1, 0}}]};

Then use p inside Rotate and Translate in Graphics. This works for raster or vector images by treating the image as a textured object rather than a raw Image.

Use patterns that match only an isolated "B", and exclude the protected region first.

One way is:

firstB = StartOfString ~~ "B" ~~ post:Except["B"] :> "oneB" ~~ post;
lonelyB = pre:Except["B"] ~~ "B" ~~ post:Except["B"] :> pre ~~ "oneB" ~~ post;
lastB = pre:Except["B"] ~~ "B" ~~ EndOfString :> pre ~~ "oneB";
leftright = lr : Shortest["\\Left" ~~ ___ ~~ "\\Right"] :> lr

Then:

StringReplace[str, {firstB, lonelyB, lastB, leftright}]

This avoids replacing "B" inside "BB" or "BBB", and leaves text between \Left and \Right unchanged.

You can avoid generating all of Tuples by using a “center line” and testing only nearby points. The suggested approach defines the inequalities as a predicate and then samples points around an approximate middle line:

lin[x_] := (x[[2]] > (1.9*x[[1]] - 4)) && (x[[2]] < (1.9*x[[1]] + 3));
line = Table[{i, 1.9 i + 0.5}, {i, -35, 35}];
points = Flatten[
  DeleteCases[
    Table[
      If[lin[Round[line[[i]] + {0, j}]], Round[line[[i]] + {0, j}], Null],
      {i, 1, Length[line]}, {j, -50, 50}
    ],
    Null, 2
  ],
  1
];

This is more tractable for larger ranges than building the full lattice first.

You can define it by matching the quadratic to a shifted-square form and solving for the shift/constant. For the 2-variable case shown, the function in the answer is:

CompleteSquare[expr_, {x_, y_}] := Module[{a, b, c, c1, c2, eqns, sols},
  eqns = Thread[Flatten[CoefficientList[c1 (x - a)^2 + c2 (y - b)^2 - c, {x, y}]] -
      Flatten[CoefficientList[expr, {x, y}]] == 0];
  Check[sols = Solve[eqns, {a, b, c, c1, c2}], Return@$Failed];
  (x - a)^2 + c2/c1 (y - b)^2 == c/c1 /. sols
]

For

f = -9 x + (3 x^2)/4 + (3 y^2)/4;

it returns

(-6 + x)^2 + y^2 == 36

So this supports your target form for that example.

The evidence only supports a workaround: instead of trying to run the `\d;` psql-style backslash command, query Vertica’s catalog tables directly. For example, to list schema/table/column details:

```mathematica
columns={"table_schema","table_name","column_name","data_type","data_type_length","is_nullable","column_default"};
SQLExecute[conn,"SELECT " <> StringJoin[Riffle[columns, ", "]] <> " FROM v_catalog.columns"];
```

So the backslash command itself is not being accepted by Vertica/JDBC here. The closest guidance supported is to use catalog queries for metadata rather than `\d;`.

You can set defaults globally for Graphics3D like this:

SetOptions[Graphics3D, Axes -> True, AxesLabel -> {"x", "y", "z"}, Boxed -> False, ViewPoint -> 2 {1, 0, 0}, ViewVertical -> {0, 0, 1}];

This makes axes appear by default and sets a default viewpoint/vertical direction similar to the image. The answer also notes that you may need to choose ViewPoint or ViewMatrix to get the projection you want, and that Mathematica’s built-in axis placement can be a bit odd, so if needed you can draw your own axes.

Use `EllipsoidQuantile` to get the ellipse, then extract its `Line` points and build a `Polygon` from only the points with x-values to the right of `cutscore`:

```wl
Needs["MultivariateStatistics`"]

cutscore = 0.5;
data1 = RandomVariate[
  MultinormalDistribution[{0, 0}, {{1, 0.6}, {0.6, 1}}], 500];

ellipse1 = EllipsoidQuantile[data1, 0.5];
e1 = Graphics[ellipse1];

line1 = Graphics[{Line[{{cutscore, -1.3}, {cutscore, 1.5}}]}];

Show[e1, line1,
 Graphics[{
   LightBlue,
   Polygon[
    DeleteCases[
     Cases[e1, Line[pts_] :> pts, Infinity][[1]],
     {_?(# < cutscore &), _}
    ]
   ]
 }],
 Axes -> {True, False},
 AxesOrigin -> {-1.5, -1.5},
 PlotRange -> {{-1.5, Automatic}, {-1.5, Automatic}}]
```

This shades only the part of the ellipse with `x > cutscore`.

Yes. The provided solution draws a circular region and colors it by angle using Hue:

With[{thin = {Thin, Opacity[0.4]}},
 RegionPlot[x^2 + y^2 <= 1, {x, -1, 1}, {y, -1, 1},
  ColorFunction -> (Hue[ArcTan[#, #2]/(2 π)] &),
  ColorFunctionScaling -> False, PlotPoints -> 100, Frame -> False,
  Mesh -> {21, 21, 10, 7, 47}, MeshStyle -> {thin, thin, thin, thin, thin},
  MeshFunctions -> {# &, #2 &, Norm[{#1, #2}] &, ArcTan[#, #2] &, ArcTan[#, #2] &}
 ]
]

It also suggests you can fine-tune labeling and details yourself.

You can approximate the circle by a polygon and push its vertices through the flow. The approach below solves the ODE for each vertex, then redraws the transformed polygon over the stream plot.

flow = Function[{t, a, b},
  Evaluate[{x[t, a, b], y[t, a, b]} /. 
    First @ NDSolve[
      {D[x[t, a, b], t] == y[t, a, b],
       D[y[t, a, b], t] == -Sin[x[t, a, b]],
       x[0, a, b] == a, y[0, a, b] == b},
      {x, y}, {t, 0, 20}, {a, -1/2, 1/2}, {b, 1/2, 3/2}]],
  Listable];

circlePts = With[{n = 100},
  Table[{0., 1.} + {Cos[t], Sin[t]}/2, {t, 0, 2 Pi - 2 Pi/n, 2 Pi/n}]];

Manipulate[
 Show[
  StreamPlot[{y, -Sin[x]}, {x, -4, 4}, {y, -3, 3}, 
   StreamColorFunction -> "Rainbow"],
  Graphics[GraphicsComplex[
    Dynamic @ flow[t, circlePts[[All, 1]], circlePts[[All, 2]]],
    {Opacity[0.6], Red, Polygon[Range@Length@circlePts]}
  ]],
  AspectRatio -> Automatic
 ],
 {t, 0., 20.}
]

The supported approach is to bypass ListPlot and use Graphics directly. That lets you draw the rectangles and the point/line overlay without relying on ListPlot’s automatic axis scaling.

A simple pattern is:

Graphics[{
  ({Hue @ RandomReal[], Rectangle[# - 0.5]} &) /@ data,
  {GrayLevel[.3], PointSize[.02], Point@data, Thick, Line@data}
},
 AspectRatio -> 0.5,
 Frame -> True
]

This gives you direct control over the aspect ratio and the geometry of the rectangles. The evidence does not show a way to force ListPlot itself to use uniform axis scaling with PlotRange -> All.

Yes. Use RegionPlot with a ColorFunction that takes x and y, for example:

cf = Function[{x, y}, ColorData["Rainbow"][Mean@{x, y}]];
RegionPlot[True, {x, 0, 10}, {y, 0, 10}, ColorFunction -> cf];

This shades the whole plane region using your 2D color function. You can replace the body of cf with your own RGB function.

For derivatives, “traditional output” usually means using finite-difference formulas. The supported examples are:

Central difference:
CDx[v_List, h_] := ListCorrelate[{-1, 0, 1}/(2 h), v, 2];

Backward difference:
BDx[v_List, h_] := ListCorrelate[{-1, 1}/h, v, 2];

Forward difference:
FDx[v_List, h_] := ListCorrelate[{-1, 1}/h, v];

If you want a more general built-in approach, use NDSolve`FiniteDifferenceDerivative for differentiation on a uniform or nonuniform grid. For example, a 2D Laplacian is formed by adding second derivatives in x and y.

If you mean a specific “traditional output” format, this material doesn’t define it further.

Yes. The supported approach is to capture the input cell with `NotebookRead@EvaluationCell[]` before the `Manipulate` evaluates, store it in a variable, and then use that variable inside the `Manipulate`:

```wl
manipulatecode = NotebookRead@EvaluationCell[];

Manipulate[
  Column[{
    Plot[Sin[x - n], {x, 0, 1}],
    Button["PrintCode", CellPrint[manipulatecode];]
  }],
  {n, 0, 1},
  SaveDefinitions -> True
]
```

This lets you print the original code even if the input cell is later deleted.

Yes. A function defined outside Manipulate can be used, but you need to make sure the manipulated variable is substituted correctly.

One working pattern is:

f[h_] := Which[h < a, 1 - 1.5 h/a + 0.5 (h/a)^3, h >= a, 0];
With[{g = f[h]},
  Manipulate[Plot[g, {h, 0, 8}], {a, 5, 10}]
]

Another is:

f[h_] := Which[h < a, 1 - 1.5 h/a + 0.5 (h/a)^3, h >= a, 0];
Manipulate[Plot[f[h] /. a -> b, {h, 0, 8}], {b, 5, 10}]

So the closest guidance is: define the function outside, then either freeze its form with With or replace the outside variable with the manipulated one.

Use indexed parts of a list as the dynamic variables. The key idea is that `Dynamic[data[[i]]]` can update `data[[i]]` directly, so you can generate any number of sliders from a list.

A pattern like this works:

`DynamicModule[{n = 5, data = Table[RandomReal[], {20}]},
 Column[{
  Slider[Dynamic[n], {1, 20, 1}],
  Dynamic[Grid@Table[
    With[{i = i},
      {Slider[Dynamic[data[[i]]]], Dynamic[data[[i]]]}
    ],
    {i, n}
  ]]
 }]]`

This supports a variable number of controls. The same approach can be extended to other controls, including checkbox grids for list or matrix data.

Use an outer DynamicModule to hold k, and disable localization in the inner Manipulate:

DynamicModule[{k},
  Manipulate[
    Column[{j,
      Manipulate[k, {k, 1, 5, 1}, LocalizeVariables -> False],
      Button["Check", Print[k]]
    }],
    {j, 1, 10, 1}
  ]
]

This makes k available at the outer level, so the Button can see the edited value.

Yes, but in Mathematica 8 the convolution may not evaluate fast enough for Plot, so you can get an empty graph. A workable approach is to compute values first and plot them with ListPlot, or simplify the convolution before plotting.

For example:
c[t_] := (c[t] = Convolve[x[d], y[d], d, t]);

arr = Array[c, 33, {-4, 4}];
dom = Array[# &, 33, {-4, 4}];
ListPlot[Transpose[{dom, arr}], Joined -> True]

A better option is:
c2[t_] := Evaluate[FullSimplify[Convolve[x[d], y[d], d, t]]]
Plot[c2[t], {t, -4, 4}]

Note that HeavisideTheta[0] is not defined.

To use Mathematica’s Convolve, your exponentials need to be supported on x >= 0, so include UnitStep[x]:

f[x_] = (1/k) Exp[-x/k] UnitStep[x];
g[x_] = (1/p) Exp[-x/p] UnitStep[x];

Convolve[f[x], g[x], x, y]

This gives the convolution, matching the usual result up to the step factor. If you mean convolution of probability distributions, the supported route shown is to use TransformedDistribution for x + y, with x and y defined by ProbabilityDistribution[..., {x, -∞, ∞}] and then take PDF of the sum.

Use Grid inside the Panel. Put the controls in separate columns and align the columns as needed:

Panel[
 Grid[{
   {Row[{"Board size: ", InputField[Dynamic[max], Number, FieldSize -> 3]}],
    Button["Clear Board", ImageSize -> All]},
   {Graphics[Circle[], ImageSize -> 300], SpanFromLeft}
  },
  Alignment -> {{Left, Right}, Baseline}
 ]
]

SpanFromLeft makes the graphic row occupy both columns. Also, setting ImageSize -> All on the button keeps it sized to its text instead of stretching to fill the cell.

Use Graphics with Rectangle and its 3rd argument to fill each cell with an image, which tiles them edge to edge.

Example:

imgs = {...9 same-size images...};
mat = Partition[RandomSample[Range[9]], 3];
Graphics[
  Table[
    Rectangle[{j, k}, {j + 1, k + 1}, imgs[[mat[[j + 1, k + 1]]]]],
    {j, 0, 2}, {k, 0, 2}
  ]
]

This makes a 3×3 grid with no gaps. If you need empty slots, Graphics/Rectangle supports leaving cells unfilled, unlike Grid or ImageAssemble. The same approach extends to any grid size by changing the table ranges and partitioning the image indices accordingly.

Yes. If the images all have the same dimensions, you can assemble them into a grid by partitioning the list into rows and using ImageAssemble. To make it re-run on demand, a DynamicModule with a button works:

DynamicModule[{x = Null}, Column[{
  Button["Shuffle Images",
    x = ImageAssemble[
      Partition[
        RandomSample[Flatten@{DarkBlueB, DarkBlueC, DarkBlueN, DarkBlueE}, 20], 5]]
  ],
  Dynamic[x]
}]]

Pressing the button regenerates the composition.

Yes. A compact approach is to attach labels to the curves with Tooltip and then replace them with Text placed near the end of each line.

One working form is:

Plot[Tooltip@{x^2, x^3, x^4}, {x, -2, 2},
 PlotStyle -> {Red, Green, Blue},
 PlotRangePadding -> 1.1] /. {
  Tooltip[{___, dir_Directive, line_Line, ___}, tip_] :>
   {Text[Style[tip, 14], {.1, 0} + line[[1, -1]]], dir, line}
 }

This uses only a short replacement rule and avoids writing a large custom labeling routine.

For just a few curves, another option is to add text and arrows manually in the front end with Drawing Tools.

Yes—using PlotLegends, a combined legend is possible here, and the accepted solution does it by supplying PointLegend inside PlotLegends, with explicit legend labels, markers, and marker sizes.

It also shows that the plots can be combined in one ListLogLogPlot with:
- Joined -> {False, False, True, True}
- PlotStyle -> {Blue, Red}
- PlotMarkers -> {AMarker, BMarker}
- PlotLegends -> Placed[PointLegend[...], {0.8, 0.7}]

So the built-in facility supports the main requirements shown: mixed list/theoretical plots, custom marker styles, custom plot styles, and a single combined legend.

If your cells contain lists of values, you can take the median in each cell first, then plot the resulting 2D grid with ListPlot3D.

For example:

dt1 = Array[RandomReal[1, {3}] &, {5, 5}];
dt2 = Array[RandomReal[1, {3}] &, {5, 5}];

{med1, med2} = Map[Median, #, {2}] & /@ {dt1, dt2};
medratios = med1/med2;

ListPlot3D[medratios, BoxRatios -> 1]

If your data are stored as {x, y, {z1, z2, z3}} entries, compute the medians in the third part and replace that part with the median or ratio before plotting.

You can’t use ContourPlot3D directly for a purely discrete integer set. The closest match is to plot the plane continuously and overlay the integer lattice points, then highlight the ones that satisfy your conditions.

For example, define the plane and filter integer triples with positive entries and k != h, then show the plane plus spheres at the lattice points:

With[{max = 2, plane = {x, y, z} \[Function] x - 2 y - z == 0},
 Module[{zz, grid, filter},
  zz = z /. Solve[plane[x, y, z], z];
  grid = Select[Tuples[Range[-max, max], 3], p \[Function] plane @@ p];
  filter[{k_, h_, l_}] := (k >= 0) && (h >= 0) && (l >= 0) && (k != h);
  Show[{
    ParametricPlot3D[{x, y, zz}, {x, -max, max}, {y, -max, max}, Mesh -> None, 
     PlotStyle -> Directive[Opacity[0.5], Gray]],
    Graphics3D[{
      {Gray, Opacity[0.5], Sphere[#, 0.19] & /@ grid},
      {Red, Sphere[#, 0.2] & /@ Select[grid, filter]}
    }]
  }, BoxRatios -> {1, 1, 1}, PlotRange -> max + 0.5, Lighting -> "Neutral"]
 ]
]

Increase max to see more integer points.

Use wrapped row/column indices and assign the submatrix directly.

If your big matrix is n×n, submatrix is m×m, and the lower-left origin is origin = {r,c}, you can do:

matrix = Table[0, {n}, {n}];
{rows, columns} = Mod[Range @@@ Transpose[{origin, origin + {m, m} - 1}], n, 1];
matrix[[rows, columns]] = sub;

This replaces the m×m block, wrapping around edges if needed. If you want a zero-filled submatrix, just use a matrix of zeros for sub, e.g. ConstantArray[0, {m, m}].

If you want to keep taking powers until the matrix has no zeros, this works:

pow = 1;
While[Min@Unitize@(cur = MatrixPower[mattest, pow]) == 0, pow++];
{pow, cur}

It returns the first power with all entries nonzero, along with that matrix.

If you only want the final matrix, use:

NestWhile[Dot[mattest, #] &, mattest, Min@Unitize@# == 0 &]

If you also want to track where a particular entry stays zero across powers, the example shown uses `Pick` with the positions of the zero entries. It stops once the matrix has no zero entries.

`NDSolve` is having trouble with the vector-valued form you wrote when the constant term is present. The supported workaround here is to write the system explicitly as scalar equations and use `Thread`, for example:

```wl
X[t_] = {X1[t], X2[t], X3[t]};
A = {{0, 1., 0.}, {0, 0., 1.}, {0., -0.156816, -0.523512}};
B = coef {0, 0, 1};

NDSolve[
  {Thread[X'[t] == A.X[t] + B], Thread[X[0] == {0, 0, 0}]},
  X[t], {t, 0, 1}
]
```

With `coef = 0`, the solution is trivial; with nonzero `coef`, it works as expected. If you want to study how the solution depends on the parameter, `ParametricNDSolve` is the suggested approach.

Use the recursive definition:

partitions[list_, l_] := Join @@
  Table[{x, ##} & @@@ partitions[list ~Complement~ x, l],
    {x, Subsets[list, {l}, Binomial[Length[list] - 1, l - 1]]}
  ]

partitions[list_, l_] /; Length[list] === l := {{list}}

This generates all partitions into parts of size l, assuming the list length is a multiple of l. For example, partitions[{1,2,3,4}, 2] gives the three pairings you listed, and partitions[{1,2,3,4,5,6}, 3] gives the ten 2-block partitions shown.

To avoid “No more memory available,” first test one complement on sample data and measure memory use before scaling up. The suggested approach is:

l1 = RandomInteger[{0, 10^6}, {10^7}];
l21 = RandomInteger[{0, 10^6}, {10^5}];
compl = Complement[l1, l21];

Then check `MemoryInUse[]` and `ByteCount[expr]` to see how much memory is being used. If one complement already pushes memory too high, revise the program to process one subset at a time rather than keeping many large lists in memory simultaneously. The evidence doesn’t give a more specific rewrite, but it does support using `Complement` and measuring memory to guide the change.

To avoid “No more memory available,” don’t let a huge computed result get stored in Out[]. If you only want to define the function, suppress the output by ending the definition with a harmless expression, for example:

f[x_, y_, d_] = Sum[Subscript[a, i, j]*x^i*y^j, {i, 0, d - 1}, {j, 0, d - i}]; 1;

This makes Out[] be 1 instead of the large expression, which can save a lot of memory. Also, setting $HistoryLength = 0 can help prevent old outputs from being retained. Clearing the function later with Clear[f] or f[...] =. may reduce memory use, but the main fix here is to avoid storing the large output in the first place.

Use pattern matching instead of testing inside If:

f[x_Integer] := StringForm["`` is an integer", x];
f[x_] := StringForm["`` is not an integer", x];

Then apply it to your list, for example:

f /@ {1, 3, Pi, E, Sqrt[2], Zeta[3]}

This will give outputs like “1 is an integer”, “3 is an integer”, and so on.

If you want a direct test, Mathematica also has IntegerQ, but note that IntegerQ[3] is True while IntegerQ[3.] is False.

The orbit paths and positions are in different units. The orbit path is in astronomical units, while the position is in meters, so the lines need to be scaled before combining them with the spheres.

A workable fix is to multiply the orbit path coordinates by 149597870700:

Animate[
 Graphics3D[
  {
   (AstronomicalData[#, "OrbitPath"] /. Line[a__] :> Line[149597870700 a]) & /@ AstronomicalData["Planet"],
   Sphere[AstronomicalData[#, {"Position", DateList[x][[;; 3]]}] & /@ AstronomicalData["Planet"], 5*10^10]
  }
 ],
 {x, 1, 10000000000}, AnimationRate -> 100000000
]

This matches the coordinate scales so both the orbit lines and spheres should display together.

Use Hold with Evaluate on just the part you want fixed at call time.

For example:

mytest[expr_, parameters_] := NIntegrate[expr*a /. parameters, {var, 0, 1}]

mytest1[parameters_] := Module[{},
  globpar = 1;
  NIntegrate[
    Hold[mytest[Evaluate[globpar*var*var1], parameters]],
    {var1, 0, 1}
  ]
]

Here `Evaluate[...]` makes `globpar*var*var1` use the value of `globpar` when `mytest1` is called, while `Hold` prevents `mytest` itself from being run too early. The note in the answer says that if `globpar` is also changed inside `mytest`, then you may need to assign `globpar*var*var1` to a local variable first and `Evaluate` that variable instead.

Use `h@x` rather than `h @@ x` when building the function, so the list is passed as a single argument to `h` and used to form the `Function`’s parameter list:

x = {x1, x2, x3};

h[x_] := Function[Evaluate[x], x + 1 // Evaluate];
f3 = h@x;
f3 @@ x

This gives `{1 + x1, 1 + x2, 1 + x3}`.

If you give standardize the attribute HoldFirst, the code works as written:

SetAttributes[standardize, HoldFirst];

Then your function can modify the input in place. A working version is:

standardize[a_] := Module[{alpha, beta, gamma, x},
  gamma = 2;
  For[alpha = 1, alpha <= 4, alpha++,
   For[x = 1, x <= 4, x++,
    beta = a[[alpha, x]];
    If[beta >= gamma,
     If[beta > gamma,
      a[[{gamma, beta}]] = a[[{beta, gamma}]];
      a = (a /. {beta -> gamma, gamma -> beta});
      ];
     gamma++;
     ];
    ];
   ];
  a // MatrixForm
  ]

Then standardize[a] works.

You can generate the table with Table, flatten it into a list of {x,y,z} points, then add random noise to each point by mapping a function over the list:

data = Flatten[Table[{i, j, i^2 + j^2}, {i, -10, 10, 1}, {j, -10, 10, 1}], 1];
noisydata = # + RandomReal[5, 3] & /@ data;

This adds a random 3-element noise vector in the range 0 to 5 to every point. If you want a different noise range, change 5 to the value you want.

Use a held wrapper around the symbol construction, so the symbol name is turned into a Symbol but not evaluated before the HoldAll function sees it.

For example:

```wl
ClearAll[holdingFunction];
SetAttributes[holdingFunction, HoldAll];
holdingFunction[a_] := Print[FullForm[Hold[a]]];

ClearAll[invokeHoldingFunction];
invokeHoldingFunction[symbolName_String] :=
  holdingFunction[Unevaluated[Symbol[symbolName]]];
```

In this setup, the closest supported idea is: construct the symbol from its string name, then pass it in a way that stays unevaluated to the HoldAll function. The evidence does not provide a finished `evaluateOneStep` implementation, so this is the nearest guidance it supports.

Put the clearing step inside the recursive definition, so it happens during evaluation, not after it finishes.

For example:

mem : f[m_] := (
  mem = f[m - 1] + f[m - 2];
  If[m > 3, Unset@f[m - 2]];
  mem
)

f[0] = 0;
f[1] = 1;

This keeps the memoized speed benefit while removing older stored values as the recursion proceeds. It still only really supports the pattern where you need the most recent results, like Fibonacci.

If you only need the tangent to a circle, you can avoid computing a slope and just rotate a fixed arrow:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[{Circle[], Rotate[Arrow[{{1, 0}, {1, 1}}], φ, {0, 0}]}],
 {φ, 0, 2 Pi}
]

For an arbitrary curve, the closest guidance here is to use the two-argument form ArcTan[x, y], which is better suited than manually handling cases like vertical tangents.

If you only need the point on the circle and the tangent, you can draw the radius and a rotated arrow in the same Manipulate:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[{
   Blue, Line[{{0, 0}, {Cos[a], Sin[a]}}],
   Red, Rotate[Arrow[{{1, 0}, {1, 1}}], a, {0, 0}]
   },
  PlotRange -> 2],
 {a, 0, 2 Pi}
]

This shows the point on the curve via {Cos[a], Sin[a]} and the tangent vector by rotating an arrow around the origin. The two-argument form ArcTan[x, y] may also help if you need the angle of a vector.

You can put the moving tangent and the marked point in `Epilog`, using `Dynamic` for the parts that change. For example:

```mathematica
f[x_] := x^2;
tangent[x_, dx_, f_] := {{x - dx, f[x] - f'[x] dx}, {x + dx, f[x] + f'[x] dx}};

Manipulate[
  With[{dx = 1},
    Plot[f[x], {x, -5, 5},
      Epilog -> {
        Dynamic@Line@tangent[x0, dx, f],
        Dynamic@Point[{x0, f[x0]}]
      }
    ]
  ],
  {{x0, 2}, -4, 4},
  SaveDefinitions -> True
]
```

This displays the point on the curve and the tangent vector/line, while avoiding unnecessary recomputation of the whole plot.

Yes—according to the answer, free-form linguistic input is handled by WolframAlpha. A simple way is to make the input field a string and pass it to WolframAlpha, e.g.

Manipulate[
 If[in =!= "", WolframAlpha[in], "Enter input"],
 {{in, ""}, InputField[Dynamic[in], String] &}
]

So for “cos x” style input, the supported approach shown is to send the user’s text to WolframAlpha rather than directly parsing it as a Mathematica Function. The evidence doesn’t show a built-in way to make InputField itself interpret free-form language locally.

Yes, conditional evaluation can be used in a Manipulate with Plot, but the conditional should be expressed in a form Plot can handle for each parameter value. The closest supported guidance is to make the plotted expression depend on c and q through an If-like choice, so one branch is used when c < 1 and the other otherwise.

A practical pattern is to place the conditional outside or inside the plotted expression, for example:
Manipulate[
 Plot[
  If[c < 1,
   {q^(0.1) c^(0.1) (0.87 + x)^6.95, 0.2 + q^(0.1) c^(0.1) (1.81 - x)^10},
   {c^(0.1) (0.87 + x)^6.95, 0.2 + c^(0.1) (1.81 - x)^10}
  ],
  {x, 0, 1}
 ],
 {c, 1, 5}, {q, 0.01, 0.99}
]

If you want, I can help adapt this to avoid plotting issues when one branch is not numeric.

Use ListSurfacePlot3D, not ListContourPlot3D. ListContourPlot3D is for contouring values; it won’t give the smooth closed surface you want.

A workable approach is:

data = Import["~/Downloads/furan-ks.sdat", "Table"];
nf = Nearest[data[[All, {1, 2, 3}]] -> Rescale[data[[All, 4]]]];
colfun = ColorData["Rainbow"] @ First @ nf[{#1, #2, #3}] &;

ListSurfacePlot3D[
  data[[All, {1, 2, 3}]],
  BoxRatios -> Automatic,
  ColorFunction -> colfun,
  ColorFunctionScaling -> False
]

You can tune MaxPlotPoints: lower values make the fit smoother; higher values fit more tightly but may look noisy.

If GUIKit isn’t required, the standard Mathematica approach is to use a Dynamic variable with InputField and read it in the Button action:

Panel[
 Column[{
   "Please provide the following coefficients:",
   "  Value for p1",
   InputField[Dynamic[p1tf]],
   Button["Submit",
     If[ImaginaryQ@p1tf == False,
       MessageDialog["The provided value is not valid. You need to enter complex value"];
       Exit[]
     ]
   ]
 }]
]

This shows the basic pattern: the text field writes into a variable, and the button checks that variable when clicked. The provided material does not show the working GUIKit equivalent, only this standard-control solution.

Use an EventHandler around the InputField and handle "ReturnKeyDown" to insert a newline into the notebook:

Panel @ DynamicModule[{input = ""},
  Column[{
    TextCell["Enter your text here:"],
    EventHandler[
      InputField[Dynamic[input], String, ContinuousAction -> True, FieldSize -> {40, 7}],
      "ReturnKeyDown" :> FrontEndExecute[{NotebookWrite[InputNotebook[], "\n", After]}]
    ],
    Dynamic@InputForm[input]
  }]
]

This makes Enter create a line break rather than behaving like the default InputField return action.

Yes: use a monotone piecewise cubic Hermite interpolant (Fritsch-Carlson style). It keeps the CDF monotone, is differentiable, and its derivative can serve as a PDF-like function.

The suggested construction is:

fcint[data_] := Module[{del, slopes, tau},
  del = #2/#1 & @@@ Differences[data];
  slopes = Flatten[{del[[1]], MovingAverage[del, 2], del[[-1]]}];
  tau = MapThread[Min, Through[{Append, Prepend}[Min[#, 1] & /@
      (3 del/(Norm /@ Partition[slopes, 2, 1])), 1]]];
  Interpolation[Transpose[{List /@ data[[All, 1]], data[[All, -1]], slopes tau}],
    InterpolationOrder -> 3, Method -> "Hermite"]
]

This was used on a sampled CDF and plotted successfully. If you need a PDF, differentiate the interpolated CDF.

If you want both lists aligned on the same set of dates, the supported approach is to take the union of all dates, then use the last available price whenever a date is missing.

For example, a function like this:

fillForCompare2[data1_, data2_, fill_:Missing[]] :=
 Module[{f1, f2, all, last = fill},
  all = Union @@ {data1, data2}[[All, All, 1]];
  (f1[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data1;
  (f2[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data2;
  f1[_] := last; f2[_] := last;
  Transpose /@ {{all, f1 /@ all}, last = fill; {all, f2 /@ all}}
 ]

This returns two same-length lists with missing dates filled from the previous price. If you want a different placeholder for the initial missing value, supply it as the third argument.

Yes. Import the file as a table, then select the columns you want for each interpolation.

For example:

a = Import["data.txt", "Table"];
f1 = Interpolation[a[[All, {1, 2}]]];
f2 = Interpolation[a[[All, {1, 3}]]];

This makes f1 from columns 1 and 2, and f2 from columns 1 and 3.

An equivalent approach is to transpose after import and build pairs manually, but the column-selection form above is simplest.

You can do this by scanning the held expression for symbols and building the `Block` list automatically. One approach is to pick symbols in `Global`` that have no `DownValues`, then use them as local variables:

```wl
ClearAll[withBlockedVars];
SetAttributes[withBlockedVars, HoldRest];

withBlockedVars[Hold[expr_], code_] :=
  With[
    {heldVars =
      Thread[
        Cases[
          Unevaluated[expr],
          s_Symbol /; Context[s] === "Global`" && DownValues[s] === {} :> HoldComplete[s],
          Infinity,
          Heads -> True
        ],
        HoldComplete
      ]
    },
    heldVars /. HoldComplete[vars_List] :> Block[vars, code]
  ]
```

Example:

```wl
x = 1; y = 2; z = 3;
withBlockedVars[
  Hold[x*y/z],
  Hold[x*y/z] /. {x -> y + z, y -> x + z, z -> x + y}
]
```

This gives `Hold[((y + z) (x + z))/(x + y)]`.

Use FileDate to sort the file names by modification date, then import in that order:

dateOrdered = ((names = FileNames["*.dat"])[[Ordering[FileDate[#, "Modification"] & /@ names]]]);

data = Import[#, "Table"] & /@ dateOrdered;

This imports oldest modified files first and the newest last. If you want the newest file only, take the last element of dateOrdered and import that.

Localize the iterator with Module:

f[x_] := Module[{i}, Table[x, {i, 1, 3}]]

Then f[i] gives {i, i, i}, not {1, 2, 3}.

You don’t need different iterator names for different constructs if they’re localized in the same Module:

g[x_] := Module[{i, a, b},
  a = Table[x, {i, 3}];
  b = Table[x^3, {i, 2}];
  {a, b}
]

Also, you don’t need Return if you end the Module with the expression you want returned.

Use a unique function name for the lookup or generate it dynamically, rather than relying on a global variable.

One approach is to precompute a dispatch table the first time the function is called, then reuse it:

elem[chem_, element_] := With[{dispatchTable = Dispatch[Thread[elements -> chem]]},
  elem[chem, el_] := el /. dispatchTable;
  elem[chem, element]
]

This lets `elem[chemistry, "Ni"]` return `0.06`.

A cleaner version is to make a function generator so the lookup function is created from the element list and doesn’t depend on a global `elements` variable:

makeElem[elemName_Symbol, elements_] := elemName[chem_, element_] := With[{dispatchTable = Dispatch[Thread[elements -> chem]]},
  elemName[chem, el_] := el /. dispatchTable;
  elemName[chem, element]
]

Then call `makeElem[elem, elements]` and use `elem[...]` as before.

The issue is in the y equation: the g term should multiply the whole bracket, not just part of it.

Use:

a = 8.0; d = 0.2; g = 0.4; m = 15; s = 0.4;
data = RecurrenceTable[
  {x[0] == 17, y[0] == 30,
   x[t] == (1. - a s) x[t - 1] + a (s m + g (s m/d - y[t - 1]) + ArcTan[x[t - 1] - m]),
   y[t] == (1. - d) y[t - 1] + s m + g (s m/d - y[t - 1]) + ArcTan[x[t - 1] - m]},
  {x, y}, {t, 0, 7000}
];

ListPlot[data, AxesLabel -> {"x[t]", "y[t]"}, PlotRange -> All, PlotStyle -> PointSize[Small]]

This should generate the phase diagram points correctly.

Use Roots with Quartics -> False so the roots stay as Root objects instead of expanding into huge formulas:

SetOptions[Roots, Quartics -> False];
a1 = Roots[x^4 + r1 x^3 + r2 x^2 + r3 x + r4 == 0, x][[1]][[2]];
a2 = Roots[x^4 + r1 x^3 + r2 x^2 + r3 x + r4 == 0, x][[2]][[2]];

If you only want them to display without the x == style, define a Format for Root, for example:

Unprotect[Root];
Format[Root[func_, v_], StandardForm] := Row[{"eq(", v, ")"}];
Protect[Root];

That changes the display, while the underlying expression remains available for computation.

Yes—nesting With[] is a reasonable approach when later local constants depend on earlier ones. That said, a common alternative is to use Module[] and assign the intermediate values in sequence with =, which can be easier to read for many dependent constants.

For example, instead of deeply nested With[], you can do:

Module[{usnoData, lineLength, lsCount},
  usnoData = Import[...];
  lineLength = StringPosition[usnoData, "\n"][[1, 1]];
  lsCount = Length[StringCases[usnoData, "\n"]] + 1;
  ...
]

Use With[] when you want pure substitution and the values are ready up front; use Module[] when you have a chain of computed locals.

If you were using `Print`, that won’t build a list because `Print` returns `Null`.

A better way is to avoid `AppendTo` and collect the values with `Sow`/`Reap`, then join them to the original list:

```wl
result = Reap[
  For[i = 1, i < 6, i++,
    For[e = 1, e < 6, e++,
      Sow[Mod[v[[i]] + v[[e]], 2]]
    ]
  ]
][[2, 1]];

v = Join[v, result];
```

Even simpler, use `Table` and `Join`:

```wl
v = Join[v, Flatten[Table[Mod[v[[i]] + v[[e]], 2], {i, 5}, {e, 5}], 1]];
```

Or use vectorized form:

```wl
v = Join[v, Mod[Plus @@@ Tuples[v, 2], 2]];
```

You can split the dataset by row position using Part with Span:

data1 = data[[;; i]];
data2 = data[[i + 1 ;;]];

This returns the first i rows in data1 and the rest in data2. If you want to split at a specific row index based on the first column value, this evidence only shows splitting by position, not by matching the first-column value.

Define the two lists, then transpose them into pairs:

xx = {0.1, 0.3, 0.35, 0.57, 0.88, 1.0};
yy = {1.2, 3.5, 4.5, 7.8, 9.0, 12.2};

data = Transpose@{xx, yy};
ListPlot[data]

This turns {{xx...}, {yy...}} into {{x1, y1}, {x2, y2}, ...}, which is the form ListPlot needs.

Extract the columns you want, pair them up, then plot the paired points.

For example:

x = data2[[All, 1]];
y = data1[[All, 3]];
data = Transpose[{x, y}];

ListPlot[data]

If you want styling and labels, you can add options like:

ListPlot[data, PlotStyle -> Directive[Red, PointSize[Large]], Frame -> True]

This plots the 3rd column of data1 against the 1st column of data2.

If you want fewer markers in `ListLinePlot`, one simple approach is to plot the line normally and overlay a `ListPlot` of only every n-th point:

data = Thread@{Range@10, Range[1, 100, 10]}
llp = ListLinePlot[data];
lp[n_] := ListPlot[data[[1 ;; Length@data ;; n]], PlotMarkers -> Automatic];
Show[llp, lp@2]

For `Plot`, markers can be added by using `Mesh` and then replacing the mesh points with a custom marker, for example:

Plot[x, {x, 1, 10}, Mesh -> 5] /. 
 Point[x : {__Integer}] :> Map[Inset["■", #] &, x]

This supports markers on a normal `Plot` directly.

If the rule is “keep a point only when its distance from the last kept point is greater than delta,” then the efficient approach shown is to scan the points once and build the result incrementally.

A compiled helper does this:

getDistantPoints = Compile[
  {{pts, _Real, 2}, {delta, _Real}},
  Module[{res = Table[{0., 0.}, {Length[pts]}], ctr = 1},
    res[[1]] = pts[[1]];
    Do[
      If[Norm[pts[[i]] - res[[ctr]]] > delta, res[[++ctr]] = pts[[i]]],
      {i, Length[pts]}
    ];
    Take[res, ctr]
  ],
  CompilationTarget -> "C", RuntimeOptions -> "Speed"
];

Then apply it inside your nested structure:

GZFastAlt[delta_, data_] :=
 Module[{ldata = data},
  ParallelTable[
   Table[getDistantPoints[ldata[[subNO, dispNo, All, ;; 2]], delta],
    {dispNo, Range[Length[ldata[[subNO]]]]}],
   {subNO, Range[5]}]];

This is the closest supported solution for cutting elements based on the distance restriction.

Yes. The supported way is to encode the restrictions as assumptions or as constraints and then use Simplify/Reduce.

For your example:

con = Thread[{a, b, c} > 1/2 + Sqrt[5/4]];

Simplify[
  Reduce[
    Flatten[{a b c (a + b + c) > 3 a b c + a b + b c + c a, con}],
    {a, b, c}
  ],
  con
]

This returns True, so the inequality holds under those restrictions.

You can also visualize it with RegionPlot3D if needed.

They are not equivalent when you are using assumptions to affect simplification, because assumptions must be stated as quantified equations or inequalities. Rules are not enough.

The guidance supported here is:
- write the conditions as equations/inequalities, not replacement rules;
- then use them with simplification functions;
- `Integrate` may not use them much directly, so try `FullSimplify` on the result under the assumptions.

So the mismatch is that “Assuming” works with proper assumptions, while “Assumptions” given as rules won’t be applied the same way.

To use the same color bar, reuse the same color function and ranges in each DensityPlot. If you want the bar colors inverted, you can reverse the vertex colors in the color bar graphic:

Show[
  Replace[colorbar[valrange, cf], (VertexColors -> x_) :> VertexColors -> Reverse@x, Infinity],
  ImagePadding -> {{20, 60}, {60, 20}}
]

This keeps the same bar structure while flipping the colors. If your DensityPlots need matching bars, make sure they all use the same ColorFunction and value range.

Yes. You can define your own color function, and in many cases you can also just supply a list of colors directly.

For discrete colors:
colors = Table[Hue@h, {h, 0, 0.7, 1/10}];
discrete[x_] := Part[colors, x];

