Complement sorts internally, so it does not preserve the original order of an unsorted list. That’s why you get {e, y} instead of {y, e}.

If you want to keep the input order, use a custom approach like the one shown:

unsortedComplement[l1_, l2_] := Reap[Module[{remove},
  Map[(remove[#] = True) &, l2];
  Map[If[TrueQ[remove[#]], Null, Sow[#]] &, l1];
  Clear[remove];
]][[2, 1]]

Example:
unsortedComplement[{a, y, c, d, e}, {a, c}, {d}]
returns {y, e}.

Complement sorts internally, so it does not preserve the original order. If you need the result in the same order as the first list, use an unsorted version instead.

For example:

unsortedComplement[l1_, l2_] := Reap[Module[
    {remove},
    Map[(remove[#] = True) &, l2];
    Map[If[TrueQ[remove[#]], Null, Sow[#]] &, l1];
    Clear[remove];
]][[2, 1]]

unsortedComplement[{a, y, c, d, e}, {a, c}, {d}]

This returns the elements in the original order, e.g. {y, e} rather than {e, y}.

If by “bring standard order back” you mean restore the original sequence after doing the complement-style renumbering, the direct way supported here is to use StringReplace with a counter and, when needed, handle bracketed sections separately.

For example, for A and B:

Module[{c},
  c[_] = 0;
  StringReplace["[AA]AB[AA]BB[AA]B[AA]CCC",
    x : "A" | "B" :> x <> ToString[++c[x]]
  ]
]

If you need bracket-sensitive behavior, use a nested StringReplace so bracketed text is processed differently from the rest.

If you mean undoing a previous transformation, this set of examples does not show a true inverse/complement operation, only controlled renumbering.

