Your attempted code only checks whether a prime divides the binomial coefficient; it does not extract the full prime powers.

A workable approach is to factor the binomial coefficient, split the factors by whether the prime is below or above k, and then rebuild the two parts:

dec[n_, k_] := Module[{num, u},
  num = Binomial[n, k];
  u = Times @@ (#1^#2 & @@@ Cases[FactorInteger[num], {_?(# < k &), _}]);
  {u, num/u}
]

This returns the part U made from prime powers with p < k, and V as the remaining factor. The example in the thread shows it used for all Binomial[17, j].

If you need exactly the intervals p ≤ k and k < p ≤ n, this is the closest supported pattern from the code shown.

