A simple way is to make a snowflake shape from random points and then assemble six rotated copies around a center.

For example, in the provided Mathematica code, flake[] builds one flake by:
- starting with a small stem,
- adding random segments with RandomReal,
- mirroring the arm,
- rotating it 6 times by Pi/3,
- wrapping the result as a Polygon.

A randomized flake generator based on that idea is:

flake := Module[{arm},
  arm = Accumulate[{{0, 0.1}}~Join~RandomReal[{-1, 1}, {5, 2}]];
  arm = arm.Transpose@RotationMatrix[{arm[[-1]], {0, 1}}];
  arm = arm~Join~Rest@Reverse[arm.{{-1, 0}, {0, 1}}];
  Polygon[Flatten[arm.RotationMatrix[# Pi/3] & /@ Range[6], 1]]
];

This gives a random snowflake-like polygon each time you call flake[].

