A good way to speed this up is to eliminate variables directly instead of using Reduce on the full system. In the example, the equations can be written more cleanly and then simplified with Eliminate:

Y = Array[y, 3];
T = Array[t, {3, 3}];
K = k*IdentityMatrix[3];
M = Transpose[{Y}].{Y} + T.Transpose[T] - K;

r = Eliminate[M == Array[0 &, {3, 3}], Join[Y, First@T]]

This leaves 7 variables:

Variables[List @@ r]
(* {k, t[2, 1], t[2, 2], t[2, 3], t[3, 1], t[3, 2], t[3, 3]} *)

So the closest guidance is: use Eliminate to remove selected variables and then inspect the remaining free parameters.

Yes. The provided approach is to generate the orthogonalized terms recursively using the Hermite polynomials and subtracting projections onto the earlier terms:

u[1] = HermiteH[1, x];
Do[
  u[n] = HermiteH[n, x] - Sum[Integrate[HermiteH[n, x] u[i], {x, -1.0, 1.0}], {i, 1, n - 1}],
  {n, 2, 45}
];

This is intended to produce terms up to at least the 45th, so it should cover the 40th, 41st, and 42nd terms. If you run into performance issues, the evidence doesn’t provide a more efficient method, only this recursive code.

