You can parametrize each circle by z1 = Exp[I t1], z2 = Exp[I t2] and integrate over the product contour. That avoids asking Mathematica to deform the contour through z = 0.

For your integrand, the accepted approach is:

ii = z1*z2/(p/z2 + (1 - p) z1 - 1)*Exp[1/z1 + z1 + 1/z2 + z2];
i1 = (ii /. {z1 -> Exp[I*t1], z2 -> Exp[I*t2]})*I*Exp[I*t1]*I*Exp[I*t2];
f[pval_] := Quiet[NIntegrate[Evaluate[i1 /. p -> pval], {t1, 0, 2*Pi}, {t2, 0, 2*Pi}]]

This computes the integral over the unit circles for both variables and excludes the singularity at z = 0 by not deforming the contour. The results shown are numerical values for several p values, though their accuracy is not fully established.

