This computes and plots the derivative of the integral efficiently.

This is explained by lexical vs dynamic scoping.

Module does lexical scoping: it renames only the symbol you explicitly localize, so expressions like z = y and w := y can still refer to the outer y, while y, v[y], and D[y^2/2, y] are rewritten to the fresh local symbol.

If you want all evaluations to use the local value during execution, use Block instead of Module. Block dynamically localizes the symbol for the duration of evaluation.

For example:
Module[{y = x}, {w, v[y], y, z, D[y^2/2, y]}] -> {y, x, x, y, x}
Block[{y = x}, {w, v[y], y, z, D[y^2/2, y]}] -> {x, x, x, x, x}

