You can’t safely have parallel kernels all writing to the same file directly; that can cause missing or partial output.

The supported workaround here is to collect results in a shared variable on the master kernel, then write them to the file afterward:

file = FileNameJoin[{$TemporaryDirectory, "info.txt"}];
OpenWrite[file];
SetSharedVariable[list];
list = {};
ParallelDo[out = Prime[i]; AppendTo[list, {i, out}], {i, 1, 10}];
list = Sort[list];
WriteString[file, #[[1]], "\t", #[[2]], "\n"] & /@ list;
Close[file];

If you need live status while running, the guidance here is that more interaction between kernels reduces parallel speed, so try to minimize synchronization.

This is usually called sampling from a discretized, truncated, or censored distribution.

A concise Mathematica approach is to use a censored normal distribution on 1–50, then invert its CDF at evenly spaced quantiles and round:

a = 50; n = 20;
X = CensoredDistribution[{0, a}, NormalDistribution[25, 7]];
numbers = Round[InverseCDF[X, Range[n]/n - 1/(2 n)]]

This produces integers spread so their discrete distribution approximates the normal shape, with more values near the mean and fewer near the bounds. In the example, the result had mean 25 and standard deviation about 6.99.

`RecurrenceTable` is running into a pattern/initialization mismatch here. The supported approach shown is to define the recurrence directly and memoize it:

g[n_, 0] := g[n, 0] = n;
g[n_, 1] := g[n, 1] = n^2;
g[n_, k_] := g[n, k] = g[n + 1, k - 1] + g[n + 2, k - 2];

Then compute values with:

Table[g[n, k], {k, 0, 10}, {n, 0, 10}] // TableForm

This handles the dependency graph by reusing previously computed values and avoids the “function called with two variables when only one is expected” errors.

Use a running total for each first element, and replace each pair with its accumulated count. For example:

acc[l_List] := Module[{reg, f},
  reg[_] = 0;
  SetAttributes[f, Listable];
  {f[x_], f[a_]} ^:= {x, reg[x] = reg[x] + a};
  f[l]
  ]

Applied to your list, this gives the cumulative counts you described, e.g. `{{2, 1}, {3, 1}}` becomes `{{2, 4}, {3, 2}}` once earlier items have been included.

Find the position of the element whose second value is closest to zero, then take the list from that point onward:

ind = First@Ordering[First@Abs[Rest[Transpose[list]]]];
list[[ind ;;]]

For your example, this returns:

{{2, -0.036704}, {56.7499, 0.95489}, {73.7349, 0.73489}, {2, -0.237504}}

This works by looking at the second column, finding the smallest absolute value, and extracting from that index to the end.

Sort the list by the numeric value in descending order, compute the total, then take elements while the cumulative sum stays within the chosen fraction of the total.

For example:

takeParetoFromList[data_, cut_: 0.8, paretoColumn_: 1] :=
 Module[{dataSort, total, elements},
  dataSort = Reverse@SortBy[data, #[[paretoColumn]] &];
  total = Total[data[[All, paretoColumn]]];
  elements = LengthWhile[(Accumulate@dataSort[[All, paretoColumn]])/total, # <= cut &];
  Take[dataSort, elements]
 ]

Use it like:

takeParetoFromList[list, 0.8, 2]

This returns the top items whose values add up to about 80% of the total.

You can generate random pairs in the unit square and then swap any pair that violates the order constraint:

RandomReal[1, {100, 2}] /. {x_, y_} /; y > x :> {y, x}

This produces pairs with 0 < y < x < 1. The accepted answer notes that the resulting points still have a uniform distribution over the allowed region. If you need a different number of pairs, replace 100 with the desired count.

You can do the same kind of elementwise transformation with a replacement rule or a map.

For a list of pairs, subtracting a constant from the second entry would look like:

{{1, 2}, {2, 4}, {2, 8}} /. {x_, y_} -> {x, y - 1}

or, using a pure function:

{#[[1]], #[[2]] - 1} & /@ {{1, 2}, {2, 4}, {2, 8}}

If you mean subtracting one list of the same dimensions from another, the closest supported idea here is to apply an elementwise operation across matching sublists in the same way.

You can replace each pair with a rule or a pure function.

For example, to multiply only the second element by 2:

{{1, 2}, {2, 4}, {2, 8}} /. {x_, y_} -> {x, 2 y}

This gives:

{{1, 4}, {2, 8}, {2, 16}}

If you want a more general form, you can use:

{#[[1]], 2 #[[2]]} & /@ {{1, 2}, {2, 4}, {2, 8}}

Both approaches let you change the constant easily.

