Reap[
  Do[
    Do[
      Sow[FindRoot[g[z], {z, j + k*I}]],
      {j, 10}
    ],
    {k, 10}
  ]
]

This captures any successful `FindRoot` outputs from the nested `Do` loops.

Your setup is likely a very hard multimodal optimization problem, so Nelder-Mead is a poor choice if you want reliable small minima. It can easily get trapped in bad local minima even with many restarts or different seeds.

The best-supported advice here is to:
- try a different global method, such as `Method -> "DifferentialEvolution"` in `NMinimize`;
- if you stay with Nelder-Mead, run it many times from many initial simplices and keep the best result;
- consider a compiled implementation if you need to solve the same problem repeatedly and care about speed.

So Mathematica can minimize it, but the issue is probably the method, not the software.

