You can approximate that by defining a wrapper for your plotting function that always appends its current options, so changing the option definition changes every plot using that wrapper.

For example:

Unprotect[ListPlot];
ListPlot[args___] :=
  Block[{$lpOptsFix = True},
    ListPlot[args, Options[ListPlot]]
  ] /; !TrueQ[$lpOptsFix]

Then set the shared defaults once:

SetOptions[ListPlot, {PlotMarkers -> {"A"}, Filling -> Axis}];

After that, plots made with ListPlot and Options[ListPlot] will use the updated shared definition. This is the closest supported way shown for making a single “packet” of plot styling control multiple plots.

Use ColorFunction in ParametricPlot and map colors by segment. If you have a piecewise function, you can count its parts and build a color list like this:

colorFunction = f;
piecewiseParts = Length@colorFunction[[1, 1]];
colors = ColorData[1][#] & /@ Range@piecewiseParts;

Then apply those colors in the plot. The same idea works for ParametricPlot when you want different colors for different parts of a curve. If you need different colors for different plotted lists, the closest guidance is to use ColorFunction or split the plot into separate pieces and give each piece its own PlotStyle.

Export the images/frames first so they all have the same raster size, then export from that list. The approach shown is:

