The supported approach is to avoid forcing the curve into y = f(x) form. Instead, parameterize the curve with a single variable and interpolate x(φ) and y(φ) separately from the parameterized data.

For example:

tb2 = Table[{φ, r[φ] Cos[φ], r[φ] Sin[φ]}, {φ, 0, 2 Pi, 0.1}];
inta = Interpolation[tb2[[All, {1, 2}]]];
intb = Interpolation[tb2[[All, {1, 3}]]];

Then plot the interpolated parametric curve with:

ParametricPlot[{inta[φ], intb[φ]}, {φ, 0, 2 Pi}]

This does not directly give an intersection-finding method, but it is the closest guidance provided for handling curve intersections/interpolated curves.

You can generate the table with Table, flatten it into a list of points, then add random noise by mapping over the list:

data = Flatten[Table[{i, j, i^2 + j^2}, {i, -10, 10, 1}, {j, -10, 10, 1}], 1];
noisydata = # + RandomReal[5, 3] & /@ data;

This creates {x,y,z} points and adds a random 3-element noise vector in the range 0 to 5 to each point. If you want a different noise range, change 5 accordingly.

If you mean finding all minima of a multimodal function, the supported advice is that a single local method like Nelder-Mead is not reliable: it can get trapped in bad local minima.

Closest guidance supported here:
- use a global method such as `NMinimize` with `Method -> "DifferentialEvolution"`;
- if you use Nelder-Mead, run it many times from many different initial simplices/seeds and collect the distinct minima;
- if you need repeated solves, consider a compiled implementation for speed.

This does not guarantee a complete list of all minima, but it is the best-supported approach from the case.

