Use StringReplace with a per-symbol counter instead of a blanket ReplaceAll-style rule. The supported pattern is:

Module[{c},
  c[_] = 0;
  StringReplace["[AA]AB[AA]BB[AA]B[AA]CCC",
    x : "A" | "B" :> x <> ToString[++c[x]]
  ]
]

If brackets matter, do a nested StringReplace so bracketed and unbracketed parts are handled differently. If you’re trying to undo a prior transformation, the evidence here doesn’t show a true inverse, only controlled renumbering.

You can force uniform spacing by transforming the x-values before plotting, then restoring the displayed labels with custom ticks.

For example, for a logarithmic-like x spacing:

data = Table[{Log[x]/Log[2], y[x]}, {x, values}];
ticks = {Log[#]/Log[2], #} & /@ values;
ListPlot[data, Ticks -> {ticks, Automatic}]

This makes the chosen x-values appear evenly spaced in the plot while preserving the original labels. If you want a different kind of “uniform axis scaling,” the same idea applies: map the data to the scale you want, then set tick labels manually.

Yes—use explicit arguments so the plotted expression can evaluate inside `Manipulate`. If your variable contains a symbol, the closest supported approach is still to make that symbol an argument of the function, then plot that function.

For example:

f[x_, a_] := x^2 + a

Manipulate[
 Plot[f[x, a], {x, 0, 1}],
 {a, -1, 1}
]

If you also need a `ListPlot`, combine them with `Show`:

Manipulate[
 Show[
  Plot[f[x, a], {x, 0, 1}],
  ListPlot[{{0, 0}, {1, 1}, {2, 2}}],
  PlotRange -> All
 ],
 {a, -1, 1}
]

Put the images in a Grid inside a Panel, using separate columns for each image. Since the images have the same dimensions, they should align naturally. For example:

Panel[
 Grid[{
   {img1, img2},
   {img3, img4}
  },
  Alignment -> {{Left, Left}, {Top, Top}}
 ]
]

If one item should span across multiple columns, you can use SpanFromLeft, but for a normal image grid just place each image in its own cell.

A compact way is to put labels on the curves with Tooltip, then replace the tooltips with Text positioned near each curve’s endpoint. The resolved example uses:

Plot[Tooltip@{x^2, x^3, x^4}, {x, -2, 2},
 PlotStyle -> {Red, Green, Blue},
 PlotRangePadding -> 1.1] /. {
  Tooltip[{___, dir_Directive, line_Line, ___}, tip_] :>
   {Text[Style[tip, 14], {.1, 0} + line[[1, -1]]], dir, line}
 }

This avoids a large custom routine. For only a few curves, manually adding text and arrows with Drawing Tools is another option.

To eliminate breaks, don’t rely on one automatic Plot3D grid. The supported approach is to build a custom sampling grid that is denser near the important max/min features and coarser elsewhere.

The guidance given is to:
- identify the boundary where the function reaches a chosen cutoff, and the ridge/minimum line you want to emphasize;
- switch to polar coordinates centered on the data’s center;
- use interpolation to trace those lines;
- generate a tailored grid for the plot.

This increases resolution near max/min regions and removes the appearance of gaps. In the referenced solution, PlotRange was also cut below 2 so the low-value region was excluded.

Assign edge costs the same way as edge weights: give the graph an EdgeCost property, then build an EdgeStyle mapping from those values and apply it with EdgeStyle or SetProperty.

If the graph already exists, you can do:
g = Graph[..., EdgeCost -> {...}];
ec = PropertyValue[g, EdgeCost];
el = EdgeList[g];
edgestyle = Thread[el -> (* map ec to styles *)];
g2 = SetProperty[g, EdgeStyle -> edgestyle];

If creating it from scratch, use the same EdgeStyle mapping directly in Graph. This only supports styling edges by the property values; it doesn’t show a special built-in EdgeCost-specific formatting function.

You can extract the sampled contour points from the ContourPlot result by converting it to its graphics form and then taking the Line primitives. For example:

```mathematica
cp = ContourPlot[x^2 + (y - 1/2)^2 == 1, {x, -2, 2}, {y, -1/2, 1}];
pts = Cases[Normal[cp], Line[p_] :> p, Infinity];
```

`pts` will be the {x, y} point lists used to draw the contour. If you want them as {x, z} or another form, transform the coordinates afterward.

You can generate the table with Table and then add random noise by mapping over the points:

data = Flatten[Table[{i, j, i^2 + j^2}, {i, -10, 10, 1}, {j, -10, 10, 1}], 1];
noisydata = # + RandomReal[5, 3] & /@ data;

This adds independent random noise to x, y, and z for each point. If you only want to add noise to the function value, use:

noisydata = # + {0, 0, RandomReal[5]} & /@ data;

Yes. If you have the variables in a list, you can use Evaluate inside Function:

variables = {a, b};
values = {1, 2};
f = Function[Evaluate@variables, 2 a + b];
f @@ values

This returns 4.

A safer approach is to keep the symbols in held form, such as Hold[a, b], so they do not evaluate unexpectedly. Then substitute the held arguments into a Function and apply it the same way.

Yes. The closest guidance here is to use a rotated arrow and drive it with the curve parameter inside Manipulate. For a circle, for example:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[{Circle[], Rotate[Arrow[{{1, 0}, {1, 1}}], φ, {0, 0}]}],
 {φ, 0, 2 Pi}
]

For an arbitrary parametric curve, the main useful point is to use ArcTan[x, y] rather than trying to compute a slope manually, since that handles vertical tangents more safely. You can then place and orient arrows along the curve using the parameter and the tangent direction.

Yes. One supported way is to draw an arrow and rotate it as the parameter changes inside Manipulate. For example:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[{
   Blue, Line[{{0, 0}, {Cos[a], Sin[a]}}],
   Red, Rotate[Arrow[{{1, 0}, {1, 1}}], a, {0, 0}]
   },
  PlotRange -> 2],
 {a, 0, 2 Pi}
]

This places the point on the curve at {Cos[a], Sin[a]} and shows a tangent-like arrow by rotation. If you need arrows along a different parametric curve, this suggests the same approach: compute the point and rotate/draw arrows accordingly.

Inside Manipulate, add the curve points and tangent vector in Epilog.

For points on the curve, use Point[...] in Epilog, for example:
Epilog -> {Red, Point[{x, y}]}

For the tangent vector, use Arrow[...] in Epilog, for example:
Epilog -> {Blue, Arrow[{{x, y}, {x + dx, y + dy}}]}

If you want several arrows along a parametric curve, sample points on the curve and draw short arrows between successive points.

Use `Epilog` to draw the point and tangent, and wrap the changing parts in `Dynamic`. For example:

```mathematica
f[x_] := x^2;
tangent[x_, dx_, f_] := {{x - dx, f[x] - f'[x] dx}, {x + dx, f[x] + f'[x] dx}};

Manipulate[
  Plot[f[x], {x, -5, 5},
    Epilog -> {
      Dynamic@Line@tangent[x0, 1, f],
      Dynamic@Point[{x0, f[x0]}]
    }
  ],
  {{x0, 2}, -4, 4},
  SaveDefinitions -> True
]
```

This shows the marked point on the curve and the tangent line/vector as `x0` changes.

You can do this by plotting the point on the curve and drawing a rotated arrow for the tangent in the same `Graphics` inside `Manipulate`. For example:

```wl
Manipulate[
 Graphics[{
   Blue, Line[{{0, 0}, {Cos[a], Sin[a]}}],
   Red, Rotate[Arrow[{{1, 0}, {1, 1}}], a, {0, 0}]
   },
  PlotRange -> 2],
 {a, 0, 2 Pi}
]
```

This displays the point `{Cos[a], Sin[a]}` and a tangent vector rotated by angle `a`. If you need the angle from a vector, `ArcTan[x, y]` may also help.

You can do it by plotting the parametric curve and overlaying a point and tangent vector that update with a slider in Manipulate. For example, define the curve and its derivative, then use a parameter t to control both the point and the tangent:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[
  {Blue, Thick, ParametricPlot[{x[s], y[s]}, {s, 0, 1}, PlotStyle -> Blue],
   Red, PointSize[Large], Point[{x[t], y[t]}],
   Green, Arrow[{{x[t], y[t]}, {x[t], y[t]} + v {x'[t], y'[t]}}]}
  ],
 {t, 0, 1}
]

This displays the point on the curve and the tangent vector at that point.

Complement sorts its result internally, so it won’t preserve the original order of an unsorted input list. That’s why you may see {e, y} instead of {y, e}.

If you need to keep the original order, use a custom filter like:

unsortedComplement[l1_, l2_] := Reap[Module[{remove},
  Map[(remove[#] = True) &, l2];
  Map[If[TrueQ[remove[#]], Null, Sow[#]] &, l1];
  Clear[remove];
]][[2, 1]]

This preserves the order from l1, so for example it returns {y, e}.

The closest supported pattern is to avoid forming a huge tensor product explicitly and instead accumulate the pairwise products:

sumlist = 0 cubelist[[1]];
l = Dimensions[coefficientlist][[1]];
Do[
  sumlist += coefficientlist[[n, m]] (cubelist[[n]] cubelist[[m]]),
  {n, l}, {m, l}
];
sumlist

A compiled version of this was described as faster and more memory-friendly than building large intermediate tensor products. If you want, I can also help adapt this pattern to your exact tensor-product-like list shape.

Yes, the supported approach is to use `ComplexExpand` and handle the real and imaginary parts separately. For example:

```wl
ComplexExpand[
  Solve[
    {
      Re[(1 - Sqrt[x - I y])/(1 + Sqrt[x - I y])] == A,
      Im[(1 - Sqrt[x - I y])/(1 + Sqrt[x - I y])] == B
    },
    {A, B}
  ],
  TargetFunctions -> {Re, Im}
]
```

This evaluates the complex expression by separating it into its real and imaginary components and can return both `A` and `B` in terms of `x` and `y`.

If you want to avoid nested With[]-style construction, build the full array with Outer using List as the head, then substitute the function head afterward.

For example:

expr = Outer[List, l1, l2, l3] ~Flatten~ {3, 2};
expr[[All, All, 0]] = fns;
expr

This produces the desired nested structure directly and is faster than first building the array with the target function and then transforming it. For the related flattening case, Outer[f, l1, l2, l3] followed by Flatten with levels {3, 2} gives the nested table form.

Yes. The supported approach is to combine the plots in a single plot command and use PlotLegends with a PointLegend. The example shown uses explicit legend labels, markers, and marker sizes, and combines everything in one ListLogLogPlot with:

Joined -> {False, False, True, True}
PlotStyle -> {Blue, Red}
PlotMarkers -> {AMarker, BMarker}
PlotLegends -> Placed[PointLegend[...], {0.8, 0.7}]

So the built-in facility covers mixed plotted data, custom markers, custom styles, and a single combined legend.

The supported approach is to make a custom sampling grid rather than relying on a single automatic Plot3D grid. For a simple cross-eyed 3D plot, use the same idea of controlling the plot sampling so the important features are sampled more densely.

What the evidence specifically supports is:
- identify the key region or cutoff line you want to show clearly;
- use a coordinate system that makes that region easier to sample;
- build a tailored grid with finer resolution where the surface changes most.

It doesn’t give a direct recipe for cross-eyed stereoscopic styling, but it does support custom grid construction as the way to get a cleaner 3D surface plot.

A simple way is to use the same 2D shape repeatedly along a 3D path, oriented by the local tangent frame. For a continuous swept object, the supported approach is:

```wl
P[a_] := {-a, a, 1/2 a (8 - a)};
path = First@Cases[
  ParametricPlot3D[P[a], {a, 0, 8}, MaxRecursion -> 1],
  Line[l_] :> l, ∞
];

figureEight = ParametricPlot[
  BSplineFunction[{{-0.25, -0.25}, {0.25, 0.25}, {0.25, -0.25}, {-0.25, 0.25}},
    SplineClosed -> True][u] // Evaluate, {u, 0, 1}, MaxRecursion -> 1
];
cs = First@Cases[figureEight, Line[l_] :> l, ∞];

Graphics3D[{EdgeForm[], TubePolygons[path, 5 cs]}, Boxed -> False]
```

If you want separate copies instead of one continuous shape, the same frame idea can be used with discrete positions along the curve.

Yes, the supported way shown is to create the plots separately and combine them with Show, so they share the same graphic and plot range:

a = ListContourPlot[data1, PlotRange -> All];
b = ListContourPlot[data2, PlotRange -> All];
Show[a, b]

To force the same scale, make sure both plots use the same PlotRange settings, and then combine them with Show. If needed, set PlotRange -> All on both before combining.

A simple way is to take the 3D object, drop the third coordinate, and draw the result as 2D. For example, for a contour surface:

cp = ContourPlot3D[..., Mesh -> None, ContourStyle -> {Directive[Red, Opacity[0.5]]}];

Graphics[
  Cases[cp, GraphicsComplex[pts_, g_, rest___] :>
    GraphicsComplex[
      Most /@ pts,
      {Directive[Lighter[Red, 0.5]], Cases[g, _Polygon, Infinity]}
    ],
    Infinity],
  Method -> {"TransparentPolygonMesh" -> True},
  Axes -> True
]

This gives a projection onto the p1-p2 plane, suitable for a simple “cross-eyed” style 3D-to-2D plot or overlaying with other curves.

Use a lookup table of seen values and filter sublists based on the element you want to test for duplication. The pattern in the resolved case keeps track of the first element of each pair:

Module[{f, g},
  g[_] = True;
  f[{x_, _}] := (g[x] = False; True);
  Cases[testcase, {_, _?g}?f]
]

If your sublists are of a different shape, adapt the pattern and the element used for tracking. If you only want exact duplicate sublists removed, apply DeleteDuplicates[testcase] first.

If you mean duplicates among 2-element sublists, the provided approach keeps only pairs whose second entry has not appeared earlier as a first entry.

For example:

Module[{f},
  f[{x_, _}] := (f[{_, x}] = False; True);
  Select[testcase, f]
]

A more optimized version is:

Module[{f, g},
  g[_] = True;
  f[{x_, _}] := (g[x] = False; True);
  Cases[testcase, {_, _?g}?f]
]

If exact duplicate sublists should also be removed, apply DeleteDuplicates[testcase] first.

You can construct and control that circular part directly with Graphics by drawing the circle as its own primitive, then manipulate its radius, center, and angles. For example:

Graphics[{
  Circle[{0, 0}, 1, {-2 Pi, 2 Pi}]
}]

If you need only a portion of the circle, use the start and end angles in Circle[{center}, radius, {start, end}]. You can also combine it with lines and points in the same Graphics expression and adjust colors, size, and position to match the image.

Use Roots with Quartics -> False so the solutions stay as Root objects instead of expanding into large formulas. If you only want to change how they display, define a Format for Root:

Unprotect[Root];
Format[Root[func_, v_], StandardForm] := Row[{"eq(", v, ")"}];
Protect[Root];

This changes the displayed form, while keeping the underlying expression usable for computation.

You can construct the symbol you want from other values, then use it in the assignment. For example:

i = 10;
cs["d", i] = 30;

This sets d10 to 30 by building the variable name from pieces.

If your question is specifically about using a variable inside Get, this evidence doesn’t show that directly. The closest supported guidance is to form the target symbol name from the variable and then assign or reference it. If the pieces don’t make a valid symbol name, it won’t work.

You can use the controller value by first assigning it to an ordinary variable inside Manipulate, then using that variable in your expression. The available material shows this pattern:

Manipulate[
 {x0, y0, z0} = ControllerState[{"X", "Y", "Z"}, ControllerPath -> {"Sudden Motion Sensor"}];
 a {x0, y0, z0},
 {a, 1, 100}
]

If you only need one axis, it also shows using ControllerState["X", ...] directly inside a Dynamic tied to a slider. It does not show putting a Dynamic@ControllerState[...] directly in Get, but the supported approach is to read the value into a normal variable first and then use that variable.

Use an explicit Function so the current element stays separate from the traced/applied arguments:

Map[
  Function[arg,
    Apply[{h1[arg, ##], h2[arg, ##]} &, {Trace[expr1], Trace[expr2]}]
  ],
  {1, 2}
]

This follows the same pattern as the prior case: the mapped value is kept as arg, and Apply supplies the extra values. If you need the trace to be taken per mapped element, put Trace inside the Function as shown.

You can do this by partitioning the interval into adjacent pairs and linearly interpolating each piece.

For evenly spaced points, the closest pattern here is to use the same idea with a list of sample values and connect each adjacent pair over one unit of x. The example function was:

createPiecewise[l_List] := Piecewise[MapIndexed[
  With[{pos = First[#2], y1 = First[#], y2 = Last[#]},
    {y1 (1 + pos - x) + y2 (x - pos), pos <= x < pos + 1}
  ] &,
  Partition[l, 2, 1]
]]

If you mean “subdivide an interval into n points,” this supports the general approach of generating points at equal steps and joining them piecewise, but it does not directly give a dedicated interval-subdivision function.

Since the matrix is 10×9 and has full column rank 9, you can reduce it to a square 9×9 system by taking the first 9 rows. Then compute the determinant of that square matrix and use Cramer’s rule to get individual components.

A supported pattern is:

solvefor[i_] := Module[{B},
  B = M;
  B[[All, i]] = b;
  Det[B]/Det[M]
]

This yields x_i without solving the whole system at once. The example specifically shows this for x1.

`FindRoot` can emit warnings when a starting point fails or the search runs into singular behavior. If you only want to suppress the warning, use:

Off[FindRoot::nlnum]

If you want the successful roots from a grid search, collect them explicitly with `Reap`/`Sow`, for example:

Reap[
  Do[
    Do[
      Sow[FindRoot[g[z], {z, j + k*I}]],
      {j, 10}
    ],
    {k, 10}
  ]
]

This captures any successful `FindRoot` results from the nested loops.

`Module` alone is not enough for interactive user input in this kind of box-generation code. The supported approach here is to use `DynamicModule` instead, so the variable can be initialized and then updated by the user.

For example:

```wl
SetAttributes[AutoInputField, HoldAllComplete];
AutoInputField /: MakeBoxes[AutoInputField[list_: {}, displayNum_: 3, imageSize_: 100], form_] :=
  ToBoxes@DynamicModule[{x = "default"},
    Column[{
      InputField[Dynamic@x, String, ContinuousAction -> True, ImageSize -> imageSize],
      Dynamic@x
    }]
  ];
```

This is the closest supported guidance for getting and updating a user-entered value inside the module-like construct.

If you want a pattern from increasing numbers, the supported approach shown is to define the recurrence directly and memoize it:

g[n_, 0] := g[n, 0] = n;
g[n_, 1] := g[n, 1] = n^2;
g[n_, k_] := g[n, k] = g[n + 1, k - 1] + g[n + 2, k - 2];

Then compute the table of values with:

Table[g[n, k], {k, 0, 10}, {n, 0, 10}] // TableForm

This lets the values build from earlier ones and avoids initialization mismatches.

If the ellipse has degenerated to a line segment, the same idea applies: extract the `Line` points and keep only the segment portion on the desired side of `cutscore`.

For a line segment, you can work directly with the two endpoints:

```wl
cutscore = 0.5;
seg = {{-1, -1}, {1, 1}};

keep = Select[seg, First[#] > cutscore &];

Graphics[{Line[keep]}]
```

If you need the part of a degenerate ellipse represented as `Line[pts_]`, use the same filtering approach on the extracted points, then redraw with `Line[...]` or `Polygon[...]` as appropriate. This supports plotting the visible segment, but does not add any ellipse-specific handling beyond point filtering.

Use the same pattern with an explicit Function so the mapped argument stays separate from the extra arguments. For a rational number, map over the rational value the same way:

Map[
  Function[arg, Apply[f[arg, ##] &, {a, b}]],
  {1/2, 3/4}
]

This produces:

{f[1/2, a, b], f[3/4, a, b]}

If you need multiple functions, use the same structure as in the updated case:

Map[
  Function[arg, Apply[{h1[arg, ##], h2[arg, ##]} &, {a, b}]],
  {1/2, 3/4}
]

You can control the default axis display for Graphics3D with options like Axes, AxesLabel, Boxed, ViewPoint, and ViewVertical:

SetOptions[Graphics3D, Axes -> True, AxesLabel -> {"x", "y", "z"}, Boxed -> False, ViewPoint -> 2 {1, 0, 0}, ViewVertical -> {0, 0, 1}];

This changes how the axes are viewed and can help set the apparent direction/origin in the rendered result. If that still doesn’t give the projection you want, choose ViewPoint or ViewMatrix more directly. If the built-in axis placement is not suitable, draw custom axes yourself.

Use a normal symbol/function form like l[p] rather than Subscript[l, p]. Store the value, export it as a compressed string, then clear it after exporting:

Do[
  l[p] = ...;
  Export["l_" <> ToString[p] <> ".mnaz", Compress@l[p], "String"];
  l[p] =.,
  {p, 1, 3}
]

To import later, read the file back and unpack the stored value. The available guidance doesn’t show the exact import command, but it supports exporting each value to its own file and restoring it from the exported string.

Use the same pattern: generate each button/toggler from a list with a function that has the right holding behavior. Keep each variable separate, then create the controls from the list.

For example:

```wl
DynamicModule[{x = 1, y = 2, vals = {1, 2, 3, 4}},
  SetAttributes[makeControl, HoldFirst];
  makeControl[var_, range_] := SetterBar[var, range];

  Column[{
    makeControl[Dynamic[x], vals],
    makeControl[Dynamic[y], Complement[vals, {x}]]
  }]
]
```

If you want a control array, the key is to generate each control dynamically and ensure the variable is held correctly. The same approach applies to buttons or togglers.

Use Module to make the variables local, and assign them in an order that respects the dependencies. A function can take multiple arguments and then compute local variables inside the Module:

functionName[arg1_, arg2_, arg3_] := Module[{local1, local2, local3},
  local1 = (* depends on inputs or previous locals *);
  local2 = (* depends on local1 *);
  local3 = (* depends on local2 *);
  local3
]

If the definitions depend on each other, put the earlier one first, or rewrite them so each local is assigned after the values it needs are available.

If you need local variables whose definitions depend on each other, the supported pattern here is to make the dependent computation a separate function and pass the local variable in as a held symbol, rather than trying to define the function inside the local block.

For example:

Attributes[f] = HoldFirst;
f[x_Symbol, t_] := x + t;

Module[{x = 0},
  f[x, 1]
]

This works because `f` operates on the local `x` from `Module` via `HoldFirst`. The evidence does not cover other ways of defining mutually dependent locals directly inside `With` or `Module`.

Yes. If you have the window of arguments in a list, you can use Evaluate inside Function and then apply it with @@.

For example, if the variables are in a list:

variables = {a, b};
values = {1, 2};
f = Function[Evaluate@variables, 2 a + b];
f @@ values

This gives 4.

For a sliding window, you’d form each window as a list and apply the same pattern to that list. A safer approach is to keep the symbols in a held form, such as Hold[a, b], to avoid unwanted evaluation, then replace the held arguments into Function and apply it the same way.

A similar pattern should work: use a HoldAll wrapper, collect the windowed arguments unevaluated, then select/evaluate the one you want.

For example, if you want to apply a function to just one item from a sliding window, define the selector to hold its arguments and extract the desired element with Unevaluated or Hold, like:

Attributes[holdChoice] = {HoldAll};
holdChoice[args__, n_] := (Unevaluated@{args})[[n]];

This supports picking one argument without forcing the others. For a normal list, there isn’t a built-in way to select one item while preventing the rest from evaluating. If your case is just choosing among alternatives, Switch is also a clean option.

Use Outer with List to build the nested structure, then replace the heads with your function, as in:

expr = Outer[List, l1, l2, l3] ~Flatten~ {3, 2};
expr[[All, All, 0]] = fns;
expr

If you want the direct function-built form, the same pattern applies: use Outer to create the nested table, then transform it. This is the closest supported method shown here for mapping onto multiple nested lists simultaneously.

If you want one expression to stand for another, the supported approach here is to construct a symbol name from the pieces and assign to it. For example:

i = 10;
cs["d", i] = 30;

This creates the variable d10 and sets it to 30.

So if your goal is to write a variable in terms of another expression, you can build the variable name dynamically from that expression and then assign its value. If the pieces don’t form a valid symbol name, it won’t work.

Export the frames as images of the same size first, then export the GIF. A working approach is:

bbox = MapThread[Max, Rasterize[#, "RasterSize"] & /@ list];
list2 = ImageCrop[Rasterize[#], bbox, Padding -> White] & /@ list;
Export["A://zod.gif", list2, "DisplayDurations" -> 3]

This makes every frame the same size before export, which avoids corruption. Then use the resulting GIF file in PowerPoint.

You can’t directly see a notebook auto-evaluate on open from this. The closest supported approach is to create a button that, when pressed, runs:

```wl
evalStart := Module[{},
  FrontEndExecute[FrontEndToken[InputNotebook[], "EvaluateInitialization"]];
  FrontEndExecute[FrontEndToken[InputNotebook[], "SelectAll"]];
  FrontEndExecute[FrontEndToken[InputNotebook[], "EvaluateCells"]];
];

Button["do it", evalStart]
```

This evaluates initialization cells first, then all cells in the notebook. If you just want a manual shortcut, use Command-A, then Enter.

To save all variables in the workspace, the supported guidance here is to write them out yourself as name/value pairs rather than using a built-in “save all” format. Open an output file with OpenWrite, then loop over the symbols you want to save and use WriteString to print each variable’s name and value, for example:

WriteString[file, "x = ", ToString[x, InputForm], "\n"];

For many variables, build a list of symbols and format each one the same way. This preserves both names and values in a custom text file.

Use Export to write the workspace contents to a file as a list or another suitable format. The supported example shows building the output filename from parts with FileBaseName, IntegerString, and <>:

For[i = 1, i < 3, i++,
  outdata = ReadList[test[[i]], Record, RecordSeparators -> {{">"}, {" "}}];
  Export[FileBaseName[test[[i]]] <> IntegerString[3, 10, i] <> ".txt", outdata, "List"]
];

If you want names and values preserved, the closest guidance here is to export the data in a format that keeps them together, such as a list.

Your code likely isn’t drawing multiple solution curves because the plotting function is treating the curves as one expression over a single parameter range. The supported approach is to use one overall parameter range and wrap each curve in ConditionalExpression so it only exists on its own interval.

For example:
ParametricPlot[
  {
