A direct example is:

cp = ContourPlot[x^2 + y^2, {x, -5, 5}, {y, -5, 5}, Frame -> False, Axes -> False, PlotRangePadding -> 0, Contours -> {4, 9, 16, 25}, ContourShading -> False, ContourStyle -> {{Thick, Red}}];
p1 = Plot3D[Sqrt[x^2 + y^2], {x, -5, 5}, {y, -5, 5}, MeshFunctions -> (#3 &), Mesh -> {Table[{j}, {j, 2, 5}]}, BoxRatios -> {1, 1, 1}, MeshStyle -> {Thick, Red}];
gr = Graphics3D[{Texture[cp], EdgeForm[], Polygon[{{-5, -5, 0}, {5, -5, 0}, {5, 5, 0}, {-5, 5, 0}}, VertexTextureCoordinates -> {{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}, {0, 1}}]}, Lighting -> "Neutral"];
Show[p1, gr]

This covers plotting 2D level curves in a 3D scene.

Use Function with HoldFirst and MatchQ on Unevaluated to stop the argument from evaluating:

test = Function[, MatchQ[Unevaluated@#, p1 | p1@_?#0], HoldFirst];

with

p1 = HoldPattern @ f[_];

Then:

f[b] = "FAIL!";
f[a]@f[b]@f[c] // test

returns True.

If evaluation is not a concern, a simpler form is:

test = MatchQ[#, f[_] | f[_]@_?#0] &;

The evidence also shows a recursive pattern-based approach using HoldAll if you want to avoid evaluation leaks entirely.

Yes. For a circle, the simplest way is to draw one arrow and rotate it inside Manipulate:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[{Circle[], Rotate[Arrow[{{1, 0}, {1, 1}}], φ, {0, 0}]}],
 {φ, 0, 2 Pi}
]

If you need the tangent direction more generally, the two-argument form ArcTan[x, y] may help compute the angle.

Yes. For a circle, you can simply rotate an arrow inside Manipulate:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[{Circle[], Rotate[Arrow[{{1, 0}, {1, 1}}], φ, {0, 0}]}],
 {φ, 0, 2 Pi}
]

If you need the tangent direction more generally, the thread also points to the two-argument form ArcTan[x, y].

You can use a parameter slider for the point and then plot both the curve and its tangent vector at that point. For example, if your curve is y[x_], compute the point a and slope y'[a], then draw the point and a short arrow for the tangent direction.

A typical pattern is:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[{
   PointSize[Large], Point[{a, y[a]}],
   Arrow[{{a, y[a]}, {a + 0.3, y[a] + 0.3 y'[a]}}]
 }],
 {a, -2, 2}
]

If you want the whole curve too, combine this with Plot[y[x], {x, ...}] and use Epilog to overlay the point and arrow.

Yes. Use Re and Im together inside Solve, and wrap the result in ComplexExpand with TargetFunctions -> {Re, Im}:

ComplexExpand[
  Solve[
    {
      Re[(1 - Sqrt[x - I y])/(1 + Sqrt[x - I y])] == A,
      Im[(1 - Sqrt[x - I y])/(1 + Sqrt[x - I y])] == B
    },
    {A, B}
  ],
  TargetFunctions -> {Re, Im}
]

This gives formulas for both A and B simultaneously.

Use the sequence as a list and test that list:

f[first_, arg___] := {arg} === {"ArgValue"};

For a general “was anything passed?” check, compare {arg} with {}. If {arg} is {} then no extra argument was given; otherwise there was at least one.

If you mean selecting different parts of a plot conditionally, the supported approach here is to build separate plots with their own RegionFunction and combine them with Show. For piecewise 1D functions, Piecewise is the closest fit:

pw = Piecewise[{
  {2 Sqrt[x], 0 <= x <= 1},
  {4 - 2 x, 1 < x < 2.5},
  {2 x - 7, 2.5 <= x <= 4}
}, Indeterminate];

Plot[pw, {x, -1, 5}]

For multiple plots with different regions, make each plot separately and Show them together. The evidence doesn’t show interactive point selection inside a region, but it does support region-based plotting and combining plots this way.

Use a DynamicModule with ClickPane to set the plot center and a control for magnification. The example below lets you click in the plot to recenter it, then choose a magnification level to zoom around that point:

```wl
SeedRandom[42];
With[{data = RandomInteger[100, 100]},
 With[{xspan = Length@data, yspan = Max@data},
  DynamicModule[{mag, center, box, plotCenter},
   Column[{
     ClickPane[
      Dynamic @ ListPlot[data,
        PlotRange -> box[center],
        Frame -> True, Axes -> False,
        ImageSize -> Medium],
      (center = ##) &],
     Dynamic @ Style[Row[{"Magnification: ", mag, "  Centered at: ", center}], "SR"],
     Dynamic @ ActionMenu["Magnification",
      {1 :> (mag = 1; center = plotCenter),
       1.5 :> (mag = 1.5),
       2 :> (mag = 2)}]
    }, Center],
   Initialization :> (
    mag = 1;
    plotCenter = .5 {xspan, yspan};
    center = plotCenter;
    box[center_] := With[{x = center[[1]], y = center[[2]], dx = .5 xspan/mag, dy = .5 yspan/mag},
      {{x - dx, x + dx}, {y - dy, y + dy}}
    ]
   )]]
 ]
]
```

This supports clicking to choose a region and zooming via magnification.

You can solve it by first finding the missing values from the first elements of the pairs, then inserting them back into the list.

missing = Complement[Range@10, b[[All, 1]]];
Fold[Insert[#1, {#2, 1}, #2] &, b, missing]

This gives:
{{1, 1}, {2, 2}, {3, 3}, {4, 1}, {5, 5}, {6, 6}, {7, 1}, {8, 8}, {9, 1}, {10, 10}}

The issue with your loop is that inserting into the list changes its length while you are still indexing it, which causes the part errors.

`Manipulate` can’t use the controller path the way you tried. The working pattern is to read the sensor values with `ControllerState[...]` and assign them to variables inside the `Manipulate`, then use those variables in the computation.

For example:

```wl
Manipulate[
 {x0, y0, z0} = ControllerState[{"X", "Y", "Z"}, ControllerPath -> {"Sudden Motion Sensor"}];
 a {x0, y0, z0},
 {a, 1, 100}
]
```

So the answer is: use `ControllerState` to get the values, not `Get`. The evidence doesn’t show using a variable directly in `Get`, but it does show that controller values are retrieved this way and then fed into `Manipulate` logic.

In Mathematica, you can build the symbol name from a variable’s value and assign to it using a held expression, for example with ToHeldExpression and concatenation.

Example:
i = 10;
cs["d", i] = 30;

This creates and assigns d10 = 30, so d10 returns 30.

The accepted approach also supports more general concatenations, as long as the result is a valid symbol name. If the name would be invalid, it returns $Failed.

You can introduce the new variables implicitly by eliminating the ones you want to solve away.

For your system, a compact approach is:

Y = Array[y, 3];
T = Array[t, {3, 3}];
K = k*IdentityMatrix[3];
M = Transpose[{Y}].{Y} + T.Transpose[T] - K;

r = Eliminate[M == Array[0 &, {3, 3}], Join[Y, First@T]]
Variables[List @@ r]

This leaves the free variables as:
{k, t[2, 1], t[2, 2], t[2, 3], t[3, 1], t[3, 2], t[3, 3]}

So the system is reduced to seven free parameters.

In Mathematica, you can create a symbol from other expressions by concatenating their evaluated forms into a valid symbol name, then assigning to that symbol. The example shown uses a helper cs:

cs[x__] := ToHeldExpression @ ToString @ Row@{x} /. {_[s_Symbol] :> s, _ :> $Failed}

Then:

i = 10;
cs["d", i] = 30;

This assigns 30 to d10.

It also works for names built from multiple parts, like:

cs[Pi, d10, "x"] = 86;

which assigns 86 to Pi30x.

If the concatenation does not form a valid symbol name, it returns $Failed, for example cs[5, "x"] since symbol names cannot start with numbers.

The issue described is that GIF export can fail if the frames are not all the same size. The suggested workaround is to rasterize each matrix, crop/pad them to a common size, and then export the resulting images:

bbox = MapThread[Max, Rasterize[#, "RasterSize"] & /@ list];
list2 = ImageCrop[Rasterize[#], bbox, Padding -> White] & /@ list;
Export["A://zod.gif", list2, "DisplayDurations" -> 3]

This is the only fix covered here. If your GIF still shows only the first frames, the closest supported guidance is to ensure every frame has identical dimensions before exporting.

Use a 2D `ContourPlot` for the circle, then lift its line into 3D at `z = 0` and combine it with the `ContourPlot3D` result. The idea is to replace each `Line[pts_]` from the 2D plot with a 3D tube built from `Append[#, 0] & /@ pts`.

For example:

```mathematica
Show[
 ContourPlot3D[
  {x^2 + y^2 == z, 2 y == z, x^2 + (y - 1/2)^2 == 1},
  {x, -2, 2}, {y, -0.5, 1}, {z, 0, 2},
  ContourStyle -> Opacity[.3]
 ],
 Graphics3D[{
   Red,
   Cases[
    Normal@ContourPlot[
      x^2 + (y - 1/2)^2 == 1, {x, -2, 2}, {y, -1/2, 1}
    ],
    Line[pts_] :> Tube[Append[#, 0] & /@ pts],
    Infinity
   ]
 }],
 PlotRange -> All
]
```

This plots the circle only in the `xy`-plane.

You can generate random real pairs and then enforce the order after generation. For example:

RandomReal[1, {100, 2}] /. {x_, y_} /; y > x :> {y, x}

This produces 100 pairs with values in [0,1], and swaps any pair where the second entry is larger than the first, so you get y < x. If you need only pairs with 0 < y < x < 1, this is the closest supported method shown here.

The provided guidance only covers changing output from scientific form to accounting form, for example by using the Formatting tab under Edit > Preferences > Appearance > Numbers, or by setting:

$PrePrint = AccountingForm;

It does not specifically address very small numbers showing as `0.`. The closest supported guidance is to change the display format of numbers via preferences or `AccountingForm`, which may help with how numbers are rendered, but no exact method for preserving tiny nonzero values is given here.

You can create a button that runs the notebook automatically by evaluating the initialization cells, selecting all cells, and then evaluating them:

```wl
evalStart := Module[{},
  FrontEndExecute[FrontEndToken[InputNotebook[], "EvaluateInitialization"]];
  FrontEndExecute[FrontEndToken[InputNotebook[], "SelectAll"]];
  FrontEndExecute[FrontEndToken[InputNotebook[], "EvaluateCells"]];
];

Button["do it", evalStart]
```

When you evaluate this, the button appears. Pressing it runs the notebook top to bottom. The thread also suggests a simpler manual method: Command-A, then Enter, to select and evaluate all cells.

The evidence only shows evaluating a list of expressions imported from a file, not operating on specific elements.

For a file containing expressions, the shown workflow is:
expr = Import["a.txt"];
result = ToExpression[expr];
Export["result.txt", result];

If you want to act on specific list elements, the closest supported idea is to import the list first, then apply operations to selected parts before exporting. The evidence does not give the exact syntax for picking elements.

If you want to remove from the first list every occurrence of elements that appear in the second list, the example shown gives {b, d, a, e} from {a, b, c, a, d, a, e} and {a, c, a}.

One listed approach is:

fastRF[a_List, b_List] :=
  Module[{c, o, x},
    c = Join[b, a];
    o = Ordering[c];
    x = 1 - 2 UnitStep[-1 - Length[b] + o];
    x = FoldList[Max[#, 0] + #2 &, x];
    x[[o]] = x;
    Pick[c, x, -1]
  ]

This is said to produce:
fastRF[{a, b, c, a, d, a, e}, {a, c, a}]  ->  {b, d, a, e}

It also notes a limitation: all elements in the drop list must be present in the main list.

Yes. You can wrap the step size in Manipulate and plot the exact solution together with the Euler approximations. The accepted approach is to use NDSolve with Method -> "ExplicitEuler" and vary StartingStepSize.

A direct pattern is:

yTrue = y /. First @ DSolve[{y'[t] == 1 - t + 4 y[t], y[0] == 1}, y, t];

Manipulate[
 Module[{ya, pts},
  ya = y /. First @ NDSolve[
      {y'[t] == 1 - t + 4 y[t], y[0] == 1},
      y, {t, 0, 1},
      Method -> "ExplicitEuler",
      StartingStepSize -> h
    ];
  pts = Transpose[Append[ya["Coordinates"], ya["ValuesOnGrid"]]];
  Show[
   Plot[yTrue[t], {t, 0, 1}, PlotStyle -> {Thick, Dashed, Gray}],
   ListPlot[pts, Joined -> True, Mesh -> All, PlotMarkers -> Automatic],
   Axes -> None, Frame -> True
  ]
 ],
 {h, 2^-5, 2^-1}
]

This lets you vary the step size and see the numerical solution approach the analytic one.

You can apply the same idea with a replacement rule or a mapped function, but with subtraction instead of multiplication.

For example, to subtract 2 from the second element of each pair:

{{1, 2}, {2, 4}, {2, 8}} /. {x_, y_} -> {x, y - 2}

Or with a pure function:

{#[[1]], #[[2]] - 2} & /@ {{1, 2}, {2, 4}, {2, 8}}

If you want to subtract one nested part from another, the evidence only supports this general pattern for transforming list elements, not a specific more complex nested subtraction formula.

You can do it by checking whether each row’s total is zero, and if so return a row of zeros; otherwise normalize it.

For your function, the key change is:

molPct[oxw_, mw_] :=
  Module[{oxcomp, divmw},
    oxcomp = Drop[oxw, None, 1];
    divmw = Transpose[Transpose[oxcomp]/mw];
    With[{tot = Total@#},
      If[tot == 0, ConstantArray[0, Length@#], 100 #/tot]
    ] & /@ divmw
  ];

This applies the conversion row by row.  
An alternative equivalent form is:

molPct2[data_, mw_] :=
  With[{tot = Total@#},
    If[tot == 0, ConstantArray[0, Length@#], 100 #/tot]
  ] & /@ (#/mw & /@ Rest /@ data)

This is the closest guidance supported.

Use the ToMatlab package. It converts Mathematica expressions, including matrices, into valid MATLAB syntax:

<<ToMatlab`
RandomInteger[5, {5, 5}] // ToMatlab

This can produce output like:

[5,0,5,3,4;
 5,5,3,0,2;
 1,4,4,4,4;
 0,3,2,5,5;
 4,5,5,1,1];

It also converts equations/expressions, though not general definitions or patterns.

Use a validity check before calling Solve, for example:

variableQ = Quiet @ ListQ @ Solve[{}, #] &;

This is a simple way to test whether an expression is accepted as a variable by Solve. The evidence doesn’t show how to directly assign Solve’s results to variable(s), but it does support checking the variable first to avoid bad input messages.

Use the listable form directly, then assign it to a list:

L = L + r;

or, if you want a new list result:

L2 = L + r;

The accepted answer shows this is faster than looping with Table, and the result is the same. If you need the output stored back into the original list, assign the expression directly to that variable.

Use list syntax around the Solve result:

sol = Solve[eqns, vars];
{sol}

or directly:

{Solve[eqns, vars]}

If you want the solutions as a list of replacement rules, Solve already returns a list in many cases. If you mean assign the output to a list element, use part assignment like:

lst[[1]] = Solve[eqns, vars]

The only option evidence here mentions is Method -> "Legacy", which forces Solve to use the older algorithm in version 8.

Use explicit symbols instead of subscripts, then replace the solved variable from the first solution:

ClearAll[eq1, eq2, fc1, sols]

eq1 = (x - c1)^2 + (tauc + Tan[philin] x)^2 == (sigmasf - c1)^2 + tausf^2;
eq2 = (x - c1) + (tauc + Tan[philin] x) Tan[philin] == 0;

sols = Solve[eq1 && eq2, {c1, x}];

fc1[tauc_, philin_, sigmasf_, tausf_] := Evaluate@(c1 /. sols[[1]])

ans = fc1[50, π/6*1.1, 1000, 800]
N[ans, 3]

If you keep using Subscript, Mathematica may not treat them as ordinary variables, which makes extraction harder and unreliable.

You can make an accumulator-like function in a couple of ways.

One simple way is to use Module with a local variable:

add = Module[{x = 0}, x += # &];

Then:
add[3] -> 3
add[4] -> 7
add[10] -> 17

You can also define a factory:

makeAccumulator[init_: 0] := Module[{x = init}, x += # &];
add = makeAccumulator[99];

Another option is to store the running total as a separate definition:

add[] = 0;
add[x_] := add[] += x;

This also lets you reset it easily by assigning a new value to add[].

To get equally spaced points, use evenly spaced angles, not random points. The cited solution instead uses a random rotation of the n-th roots on the circle, which produces n equally spaced points:

randomCirclePoints[n_, center_, radius_] := Module[{θ},
  θ = RandomReal[{0, 2 Pi}];
  Table[
    center + radius {Cos[θ + 2 Pi k/n], Sin[θ + 2 Pi k/n]},
    {k, 0, n - 1}
  ]
]

This gives n points on the circle, all separated by the same arc length. If you want a circle centered at {x, y} with radius r, pass {x, y} as center and r as radius.

Use an outer DynamicModule to store the variables, and make the controls inside Manipulate not localize those variables. The key idea is that the button needs access to the same variable that the slider edits.

For one variable, the pattern is:

DynamicModule[{k},
  Manipulate[
    Column[{j,
      Manipulate[k, {k, 1, 5, 1}, LocalizeVariables -> False],
      Button["Check", Print[k]]
    }],
    {j, 1, 10, 1}
  ]
]

For n variables with n buttons, the supported idea is the same: put the variables in an outer DynamicModule and disable localization in the inner controls so each button can see the edited value.

If you want the output of a `While` loop in a list, don’t use `Print`, since `Print` returns `Null` and won’t collect values.

Use `Sow`/`Reap` inside the loop, then take the reaped values:

```wl
result = Reap[
  While[condition,
    Sow[expression];
    (* update condition variables *)
  ]
][[2, 1]];
```

Then you can join it to an existing list if needed:

```wl
v = Join[v, result];
```

If your loop pattern is simple, a `Table`-based approach may be easier than `While`.

To make a GIF from a complex Manipulate, export the frames first as same-sized images, then combine them into the GIF. The working approach is:

bbox = MapThread[Max, Rasterize[#, "RasterSize"] & /@ list];
list2 = ImageCrop[Rasterize[#], bbox, Padding -> White] & /@ list;
Export["A://zod.gif", list2, "DisplayDurations" -> 3]

The key is cropping/padding each frame to a common size before exporting. This avoids corruption and should work for PowerPoint use.

The supported pattern here is to use StringReplace with rules that match the text you want to change, then assign new text based on a counter or other logic.

Example:

Module[{c},
  c[_] = 0;
  StringReplace["[AA]AB[AA]BB[AA]B[AA]CCC",
    x : "A" | "B" :> x <> ToString[++c[x]]
  ]
]

This shows how to take a variable-like match from a string and reassign its value in the replacement. If you need bracketed parts treated differently, use nested StringReplace. If you mean extracting a variable and updating an external symbol, that isn’t shown here.

If you want your function to behave more like Plot, the closest guidance here is to accept a list of numeric point rules and render it with ListPlot plus Epilog labels. The example defines:

plot[a : {{x -> _?NumericQ, y -> _?NumericQ} ..}] := Show[
  ListPlot[#, PlotStyle -> AbsolutePointSize[5],
    Epilog -> MapIndexed[
      Inset[StyleForm["Point " ~~ ToString@#2[[1]], FontSize -> 9], #1 + .1] &,
      #
    ]
  ] & @ ({x, y} /. a)
]

This automatically handles any number of points. If you mean Plot-like symbolic arguments or automatic plotting behavior beyond point lists, that isn’t covered here.

If you have multivariate data that cannot be inverted cleanly as y(x), the supported approach here is to keep a parameter and interpolate each coordinate separately. In the example, a curve is stored as {parameter, x, y}, then x(φ) and y(φ) are interpolated independently:

tb2 = Table[{φ, r[φ] Cos[φ], r[φ] Sin[φ]}, {φ, 0, 2 Pi, 0.1}];
inta = Interpolation[tb2[[All, {1, 2}]]];
intb = Interpolation[tb2[[All, {1, 3}]]];

Then use ParametricPlot[{inta[φ], intb[φ]}, {φ, 0, 2 Pi}].

This avoids trying to invert a multivariate interpolating function directly. The provided material does not give a direct inversion method.

Use separate `Dynamic` string variables for each field, e.g. `input1`, `input2`, etc., and wrap each `InputField` in its own `EventHandler` if you want Return to insert a newline. The supported pattern is:

```wl
DynamicModule[{input1 = "", input2 = ""},
 Column[{
   InputField[Dynamic[input1], String],
   InputField[Dynamic[input2], String]
 }]
]
```

If you need free-form text entry with Return adding a newline, use `EventHandler` around each field and handle `"ReturnKeyDown"` with `NotebookWrite[...]` as in the example.

You can generate new names from the existing function heads, then replace those heads.

For example:

```mathematica
funcs = {G[x_, y_], F[x_, y_], C[x_, y_]};
newnames = Symbol["d" <> SymbolName[#]] & /@ (Head /@ funcs);
funcs[[All, 0]] = newnames;
funcs
```

This produces:

```mathematica
{dG[x_, y_], dF[x_, y_], dC[x_, y_]}
```

If you only need the names, use `newnames`. You can also change the prefix or add a suffix by adapting the same pattern.

Yes. The supported approach is to use PlotLegends with an explicit PointLegend, and to combine the plots in one ListLogLogPlot. The example uses:

Joined -> {False, False, True, True}
PlotStyle -> {Blue, Red}
PlotMarkers -> {AMarker, BMarker}
PlotLegends -> Placed[PointLegend[...], {0.8, 0.7}]

That covers mixed list/theoretical plots, custom markers, custom styles, and one combined legend. If your issue is specifically with Subscript, Overscript, or Superscript in the labels, this evidence does not show that directly, but it does support the general legend setup.

Use ordinary symbols instead of Subscript, Overscript, or Superscript objects when calling Solve. Those formatted forms can interfere with solving and replacement.

For example, define variables like c1, x, not Subscript[c, 1]. Then you can keep Solve’s output as a list and extract values cleanly:

sols = Solve[eq1 && eq2, {c1, x}];
c1Values = c1 /. sols;
xValues = x /. sols;

If you want just the first solution as a list:

firstSol = {c1, x} /. First[sols]

This is the closest guidance supported: avoid using formatted symbols in the solve/replacement pattern.

A simple approach is to flatten by one level at a time, or all the way down if needed. In Mathematica/Wolfram Language, Flatten[list] removes one level of nested lists, while Flatten[list, Infinity] flattens all nested arrays completely.

For example:
Flatten[{{1, 2}, {3, {4, 5}}}] gives {1, 2, 3, {4, 5}}
Flatten[{{1, 2}, {3, {4, 5}}}, Infinity] gives {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}

If you want the “highest order” meaning fully flattening nested arrays, Flatten[..., Infinity] is the simplest form.

You can make the button run an evaluation function, then call that from the button:

```wl
evalStart := Module[{},
  FrontEndExecute[FrontEndToken[InputNotebook[], "EvaluateInitialization"]];
  FrontEndExecute[FrontEndToken[InputNotebook[], "SelectAll"]];
  FrontEndExecute[FrontEndToken[InputNotebook[], "EvaluateCells"]];
];

Button["do it", evalStart]
```

If your expression contains `Get`, the supported guidance here is to evaluate the notebook cells through the front end, which will also run initialization cells first. A manual equivalent is Command-A, then Enter.

If your function returns multiple values, don’t try to plot them all from one Plot call directly. Instead, generate one Plot per value with Table, like:

Table[
  Plot[{yourFunctionForM[M, x], f[x]}, {x, -1, 1}],
  {M, {10, 20, 50}}
]

If MathieuCharacteristicA is one of the multiple returned values, exclude it from the list before plotting or build the plot from only the values you want. The supported pattern shown here is to use Table to create separate plots for each case.

If you mean the extra space around a 2D plot or chart, the supported fix is to set the plot range padding to zero:

PlotRangePadding -> {0, 0}

This removes the gap between the frame and the chart area. If you’re asking about a different part to omit, the provided information only covers removing that padding space, not arbitrary sections of a plot.

I can only confirm a related workaround for aligning a ListPlot with chart elements: use the DistributionChart’s frame ticks to build a rescaling function, then plot the points with that transformed x-coordinate.

The supported example is:

k = DistributionChart[Table[RandomReal[NormalDistribution[i, .2], 100], {i, 1, 20}],
  Method -> {"BoxWidth" -> "Fixed"}];
l = (Sort@((FrameTicks /. Options[k, "FrameTicks"])[[2, 1, All, 1]]))[[2 ;; -2]];
f = Interpolation[Transpose[{Range@Length@l, l}]];
Show[k, ListPlot[Table[{f@i, i}, {i, 15}], PlotStyle -> Directive[Red, PointSize[Large]]]]

This does not directly address a broken or snipped axis in ListPlot. It only supports rescaling points to match another chart.

If you have multiple datasets, clean each one first by filtering out missing values, then plot them. The same approach can be used in a grid by applying it to each dataset before combining the plots.

For example, if one point is missing:
data1 = {{1, 1}, {2, 2}, {3, Missing[]}, {4, 4}};
clean = Select[data1, #[[2]] =!= Missing[] &];
ListLinePlot[clean, Joined -> True, Mesh -> Full, PlotMarkers -> Automatic]

For a grid of plots, make each plot from its cleaned dataset, then arrange them in a grid.

If you want to replace values that match a criterion, you can update the list/rules with a replacement rule, for example:

data = {a -> 3, ab -> 2, ac -> 1, ad -> 0};
data = data /. Rule[a, _] :> Rule[a, newValue];

If this is done inside a function and `data` is meant to be shared outside the function, assign back to `data` and optionally return the result. Then `a /. data` will give the updated value.

If you need to replace only certain entries, the supported pattern shown is matching with `Rule[a, _] :> ...`.

In Mathematica, define the function with explicit argument patterns, for example:

functionName[arg1_, arg2_, arg3_] := Module[{local1, local2, local3},
  local1 = (* routine 1 *);
  local2 = (* routine 2 *);
  local3 = (* routine 3 *);
  local3
]

You can use any number of arguments, and Module gives you local variables. If the number of variables changes, the supported guidance here is to define the function with the arguments you need and put the repeated work inside the function rather than at top level. You can also define local helper functions inside Module.

Use Outer with List, then replace the heads for the second case.

For a 3-list case:
expr = Outer[List, l1, l2, l3] ~Flatten~ {3, 2};
expr[[All, All, 0]] = {f, g, h, i};
expr

This gives the mixed-function result directly. For best performance, build the second array directly with List in Outer rather than creating the first array and transforming it.

Mathematica can’t directly make a function return a variable number of outputs based on how it’s called, but a workaround is to use a helper variable like `nargout` and `Set` upvalues to detect the left-hand side.

If by “keep function range as a variable” you mean the number of returned values should vary, the supported pattern is:

```mathematica
ClearAll[f, ff]
f /: Set[arg_Symbol, f[x_]] := Block[{nargout = 1}, arg = ff[x]];
f /: Set[args_List, f[x_]] := Block[{nargout = Length[args]}, args = ff[x]];
f[arg_] /; FreeQ[Stack[], Set] := Block[{nargout = 1}, ff[arg]];
ff[x_] := Table[x, {nargout}]
```

Then `f[5]`, `a = f[5]`, `{a, b} = f[5]`, etc. can return different lengths.

Yes. If you want the function index range kept as a variable, define the range once and use it in the iterator lists. For example:

range = Range[3, 5];

MapThread[
  (Subscript[C, #1][x, y] :=
    Subscript[q, 2 #1 - 1] + x Subscript[q, 2 #1] -
    (Subscript[q, #2] + y Subscript[q, #2 + 1])) &,
  {range, Range[Length[range]]}
]

This keeps the function range in a variable while still pairing the indices correctly.

Define the function range as a variable in Mathematica by assigning the expression to a symbol, for example:

q[x_] := Erfc[x/Sqrt[2]]/2;

If you want it available every time you open the notebook, place the definition in an initialization cell and set it to run on startup. This keeps the function definition reusable as a variable-like symbol.

You can store standard plot settings globally by setting FrontEnd-level graphics box defaults with SetOptions on $FrontEnd, for example:

SetOptions[$FrontEnd,
  GraphicsBoxOptions -> {AxesStyle -> Directive[FontFamily -> "Arial"]}
]

This affects graphics objects that do not override those defaults. If you only want it for the current session, use $FrontEndSession instead. For settings that apply only to a specific plotting function, SetOptions[Plot, ...] is also supported.

If your first query is getting bogged down because you’re working with SQL column names as strings, the supported approach is to convert those names to symbols first:

colNames = ToExpression /@ SQLColumnNames[conn, SQLTable[devFactorTable]][[All, 2]];

Then build rules from those symbols:

asRules = Rule @@@ Transpose[{colNames, Range[Length@colNames]}];
newList[col_] := devFactors[[All, col /. asRules]];

This lets you use something like newList[LossFactor] instead of newList["LossFactor"], as long as the SQL names are valid Wolfram Language symbol names.

The supported approach is to avoid global reassignment of ordinary symbols after they become lists. Use indexed storage like var[i] or an Association instead.

For example:
var["aaa"] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
var["aaa"]

If you have names = {"aaa", "bbb", "ccc"} and index = 1, use:
var[names[[index]]] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

If you need an array-like container, an Association is the closest fit:
asc = <||>;
asc["aaa"] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

This keeps the name separate from the stored value.

The supported approach shown is to use Framed@TextCell instead of Framed@txt, and place that inside Inset within Graphics. That lets the text wrap and be formatted while you control the width. For example:

Graphics[{Inset[
  Framed@TextCell[Style[txt, LineIndent -> 0, TextAlignment -> Left], "Text",
    CellMargins -> {{200, 200}, {10, 10}}], {0, 0}, {0, 0}, {w, h}
]}]

This evidence does not show a reliable way to automatically calculate the exact height for arbitrary wrapped text, so it only partially solves the “fit exactly” part.

The provided material doesn’t address adding a zero sub-matrix directly. It only supports the related point that Mathematica can mis-handle matrices with tiny numeric entries or symbolic uncertainty about invertibility.

Closest supported guidance: if your larger matrix contains very small values, Mathematica may treat it as singular or unstable. A practical fix is to rationalize the entries before doing matrix operations, for example:

c = Rationalize[Cttt, 10^(-50)];
e = Rationalize[E0, 10^(-50)];
k = Inverse[c.e.Transpose[c]];

If you want, I can help you form a block matrix with a zero sub-matrix in Mathematica.

Use an `InputField` bound to a `Dynamic` string variable. If you want free-form text, make it a string input. If you also need Return to insert a newline, wrap the `InputField` in `EventHandler` and handle `"ReturnKeyDown"`.

Example:

```wl
DynamicModule[{input = ""},
 Column[{
   EventHandler[
    InputField[Dynamic[input], String, ContinuousAction -> True, FieldSize -> {40, 7}],
    "ReturnKeyDown" :> FrontEndExecute[{NotebookWrite[InputNotebook[], "\n", After]}]
   ],
   Dynamic@InputForm[input]
 }]
]
```

A compact way is to build it as a 3D tensor and then swap the last two levels:

Transpose[
  {{{1, 2, 3}, {3, 2, 1}}, {{2, 3, 1}, {2, 1, 3}}},
  {1, 3, 2}
]

That reorders the middle object so the vectors contract naturally on both sides. For example:

{2, 4}.Transpose[{{{1, 2, 3}, {3, 2, 1}}, {{2, 3, 1}, {2, 1, 3}}}, {1, 3, 2}].{6, 8}

gives {172, 160, 172}.

If you want, I can also show a more “constructed” form using Map/Outer instead of manually listing the tensor.

If you mean a phase-space vector field built from discrete convolution, the supported guidance here is only about using Mathematica’s Convolve with functions supported on x >= 0 by including UnitStep[x]. For example:

