Use separate plots for the ranges you want to keep, then combine them with Show. For example:

a = Plot[Cos[x], {x, 0, 60}, PlotStyle -> Blue];
b = Plot[Sin[x], {x, 60, 90}, PlotStyle -> Red];
Show[a, b, PlotRange -> All]

This is the supported way to exclude a range of numbers from one plot and still display everything in one graph. If you need to skip more intervals, make additional plots for each allowed range and combine them the same way.

The same idea applies: use an explicit Function so the value being mapped stays separate from any values produced inside the body.

For example:

Map[
  Function[arg,
    Apply[{RandomReal[], RandomReal[]} &, {arg}]
  ],
  {1, 2}
]

More generally, if RandomReal is appearing in a Block/Map/Apply setup, it will be evaluated where it is placed in the expression. To keep it tied to each mapped element, wrap the mapped value in Function and build the expression inside that function.

A workable way is to wrap the Mathematica code in a small Wolfram script and call that script from Python with subprocess.

Create a script like `runMath`:

#!/usr/bin/env wolframscript
value = ToExpression[$ScriptCommandLine[[2]]];
Print[value];

Make it executable and available on your PATH. Then use a Python wrapper that calls it and passes the Mathematica expression as an argument.

Examples:
- `run Prime[100]` → `541`
- `run 'Integrate[Log[x],x]'` → `-x + x*Log[x]`

For older Mathematica versions, use `MathematicaScript -script` instead of `wolframscript`.

If your Graphics3D is made of Polygon objects, you can extract the polygon vertices like this:

p1 = addHatToL /@ Take[Flatten[Take[LsOnDodecahedron, All]], All];
surfacepoints = Flatten[
  Flatten[Cases[Flatten[p1[[#]]], Polygon[m_] -> m], 1] & /@ Range[Length[p1]],
  1
];

This gives the points on the surface of the object by collecting the vertex coordinates from each Polygon. It does not directly determine whether arbitrary 3D points are inside the object, but it does extract the polygon coordinates.

If you want a stippling/dot-shading style for a complex-region image, the supported approach here is to map the region with ComplexMap[Exp] and ensure the source strip spans a full 2π interval in the imaginary direction, such as -π <= Im[z] <= π. That will produce the image of the region rather than a blank plot.

For example:

strip = ComplexRegionDraw[0 <= Re[z] <= 1, {z, -2 - Pi I, 2 + Pi I}];
image = Draw2D[{Opacity[0.6], strip // ComplexMap[Exp]},
  Frame -> True, Axes -> True, PlotRange -> 4, FrameLabel -> {Re, Im}, PlotLabel -> w];

This does not specifically show a dot-shading effect by itself, but it is the closest supported way to render the mapped complex region.

You can combine two ArrayPlots by multiplying them, which blends the colors into new shades. If you want a stippling/dot-shading look, the closest supported approach is to work at the raster level and keep the graphics options:

gr = Table[
  ArrayPlot[RandomReal[{0, 1}, {5, 5}],
    ColorFunction -> (Blend[{White, color}, #] &)],
  {color, {Yellow, RGBColor[0, 0.6, 1]}}
];

Graphics[
  Raster[gr[[1, 1, 1]]*gr[[2, 1, 1]]],
  Options[gr[[1]]]
]

This combines the plots into one by blending their raster data.

The slow part is likely building the table incrementally inside the loop. AppendTo is especially slow because it repeatedly copies the list.

A faster approach is to collect results with Sow/Reap, or use Table and Join instead of nested For loops.

For example, build the new rows first:

result = Reap[
  For[i = 1, i < 6, i++,
    For[e = 1, e < 6, e++,
      Sow[Mod[v[[i]] + v[[e]], 2]]
    ]
  ]
][[2, 1]];

v = Join[v, result]

If you’re selecting/rearranging an existing table, try to do it in one expression rather than step-by-step inside the loop.

Use a normal symbol/function form like `l[p]` rather than `Subscript[l, p]`. You can build the list with `Table`, then export it, and clear it afterward:

`Do[l[p] = Table[Subscript[l, p, i], {i, 10}];
Export["l_" <> ToString[p] <> ".mnaz", Compress@l[p], "String"];
l[p] =., {p, 1, 3}]`

This stores each `l[p]` in its own file, such as `l_1.mnaz`, `l_2.mnaz`, and `l_3.mnaz`, and lets you import them later. Using a temporary variable would be simpler, but this matches your `l[p]` style.

If your nested loops are using Print or another side-effect inside the loop, that won’t produce a result you can reuse. Print returns Null, so nothing gets collected.

Use Sow/Reap to gather the outputs, or use Table to build them directly. For example:

result = Reap[
  For[i = 1, i < 6, i++,
    For[e = 1, e < 6, e++,
      Sow[Mod[v[[i]] + v[[e]], 2]]
    ]
  ]
][[2, 1]];

Then combine with your original list using Join.

If you want, I can also show the Table version, which is usually cleaner than nested For loops.

If you want the output of the previous line to feed into the next one, the supported approach is to extract the plotted line data from the graphics primitives and then use that data directly.

For a single plot:
data = Cases[Plot[Sin@x, {x, 0, 2 Pi}], Line[data_] :> data, -4, 1][[1]];

Then you can use data in the next expression, or export it:
Export["file.txt", data, "Table"]

For multiple curves:
gr = Plot[{Sin@x, Cos@x, Sinc@x}, {x, 0, 2 Pi}];
multidat = Cases[First @ gr, Line[data_] :> data, -4];

If you mean something different by “previous line,” this only covers using extracted plot data from the preceding plot output.

If you want output to continue on the same line, use WriteString instead of Print. Print automatically adds a newline, but WriteString writes exactly what you specify. So you can omit “\n” until you want the line to end.

Example:

WriteString["stdout", "First part: ", DateString[]];
(* some calc *)
WriteString["stdout", " -- more output: ", DateString[], "\n"];

If you specifically need a Line[] with no end, the closest supported approach is to avoid Print’s automatic newline and control line endings yourself with WriteString.

If you’re trying to change the default font for Graphics or Plot, the supported approach here is to use BaseStyle rather than $DefaultFont. For example:

SetOptions[Graphics, BaseStyle -> {FontFamily -> "Helvetica", Bold}];
SetOptions[Plot, BaseStyle -> {FontFamily -> "Helvetica", Bold}];

This applies to all text in the graphic, including tick labels. The guidance available here does not show how to change a global default font for everything, but it does recommend BaseStyle as the reliable way to control font appearance.

The supported guidance is to avoid chopping tiny values to zero before inversion. If small imaginary or numeric parts are being removed, Mathematica may treat the matrix as singular or alter the result. The closest fix supported here is to rationalize the entries first, then compute the inverse, e.g.:

c = Rationalize[Cttt, 10^(-50)];
e = Rationalize[E0, 10^(-50)];
k = Inverse[c.e.Transpose[c]];

So the issue is not that matrix multiplication or inverse is inherently wrong, but that numerical handling/chopping can cause problems.

