You can’t make a true tangent line “move along” a curve just with a static Line; you need to recompute its position as the parameter changes. In the accepted approach, the moving objects are updated inside Manipulate using the current value s, and the line segment is placed with fixed coordinates in Epilog.

For a moving tangent line, use the curve’s derivative at the current point and define the line from the point:
- point: {s, f[s]}
- slope: f’[s]
- tangent segment: {{s - a, f[s] - a f’[s]}, {s + a, f[s] + a f’[s]}}

Then put that in Epilog and animate s. If you want, I can adapt this directly to your specific curve.

A fast approach is to use UnitStep on v1:

vL = UnitStep[v1];
vL*v1 + (1 - vL)*v2

A slightly faster variant is:

vL = UnitStep[v1];
vL (v1 - v2) + v2

Using Subtract[v1, v2] instead of v1 - v2 can help a bit more:

vL Subtract[v1, v2] + v2

The evidence also shows a sparse-array replacement method can be competitive:

pos = SparseArray[UnitStep[-v1]]["AdjacencyLists"];
test = v1;
test[[pos]] = v2[[pos]];

Among the compared methods, UnitStep-based expressions were fastest; Boole was slowest.

Use two plots: one shaded from the left up to the vertical line, and one unfilled from the line to the right. Then draw the vertical line only from the x-axis to the curve height at that x-value.

For example:

Manipulate[
  dist = PDF[NormalDistribution[5,1], #] &;
  p1 = Plot[dist@x, {x, 0, l}, Filling -> Axis, PlotRange -> {{0,10}, All}];
  p2 = Plot[dist@x, {x, l, 10}, Filling -> None, PlotRange -> {{0,10}, All}];
  Show[p1, p2,
    Epilog -> {Orange, Line[{{l, 0}, {l, dist@l}}]},
    PlotLabel -> Style[Row[{"l=", NumberForm[dist@l, {\[Infinity], 2}]}], 20]
  ]
, {{l, 5.}, 0, 10}]

This matches the region under the curve and to the right/left of the chosen vertical line as needed.

Yes. Use Evaluate when building the Function from a variable list:

variables = {a, b};
values = {1, 2};
f = Function[Evaluate @ variables, 2 a + b];
f @@ values

This gives 4.

If you want to avoid issues when a and b already have values, store them in Hold instead of a normal list:

{a, b} = {"fail!", "fail!"};
variables = Hold[a, b];
values = {1, 2};
f = variables /. _[vars__] :> Function[{vars}, 2 a + b];
f @@ values

This also returns 4.

To create a GIF from a complex Manipulate, export the frames first as same-sized images, then combine them into the GIF.

A working approach is:
bbox = MapThread[Max, Rasterize[#, "RasterSize"] & /@ list];
list2 = ImageCrop[Rasterize[#], bbox, Padding -> White] & /@ list;
Export["A://zod.gif", list2, "DisplayDurations" -> 3]

The important part is making every frame the same size by cropping/padding before export. This avoids corruption and should work for PowerPoint use.

Use separate Dynamic string variables for each field, such as input1 and input2, and give each InputField its own Dynamic. Example:

DynamicModule[{input1 = "", input2 = ""},
 Column[{
   InputField[Dynamic[input1], String],
   InputField[Dynamic[input2], String]
 }]
]

If you want Return to insert a newline in free-form text entry, wrap each InputField in an EventHandler and handle "ReturnKeyDown" with NotebookWrite[...] as in the example.

Use an outer DynamicModule to hold the variables, and make the inner controls not localize those variables. Then each Button can read the same variable that the slider or other control updates.

For example, the supported pattern is:

DynamicModule[{k},
  Manipulate[
    Column[{j,
      Manipulate[k, {k, 1, 5, 1}, LocalizeVariables -> False],
      Button["Check", Print[k]]
    }],
    {j, 1, 10, 1}
  ]
]

For n variables and n buttons, apply the same idea: store all variables in the outer DynamicModule, and disable localization in the inner controls so the buttons access the edited values.

Yes, but the supported example is conditional filling between two plotted curves, not a true continuous gradient fill. You can plot both curves together and use a filling style that fills only where one is above or below the other, such as:

Plot[{curve1, curve2}, {H, 0, 700},
 PlotStyle -> {Directive[Black, Thick], Black},
 Filling -> {1 -> {{2}, {Yellow, Transparent}}}
]

This fills the region between the curves with a solid color and leaves the rest unfilled. If you want a gradient effect, the evidence doesn’t show a direct method, but it does suggest you can adapt the same idea to a family of lines or varying shading by parameter.

If you want a value supplied by the user to be available inside a module, define it in the module’s local variables and then use it when evaluating the file or code.

For example:

`Module[{a},
  a = Input["Enter value for a:"];
  Evaluate@Get["file"];
]`

Or, if you just need the value local to the operation:

`Block[{a},
  a = Input["Enter value for a:"];
  Get["file"];
]`

This supports making the variable available before the imported file is read.

If you only need an arrowed circular arc, the closest supported approach is to place the arrow on a circular graphic and rotate it by a parameter, rather than computing slopes manually. For example:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[{Circle[], Rotate[Arrow[{{1, 0}, {1, 1}}], φ, {0, 0}]}],
 {φ, 0, 2 Pi}
]

If your arc is more general, the only additional guidance available here is to use ArcTan[x, y] for direction, since it is more robust than handling vertical tangents or other special cases by hand.

If you mean “reflecting” the Polya-Aeppli distribution in the same way as a phase-space/vector-field style convolution, the only supported guidance here is the Mathematica approach using Convolve on functions supported for x >= 0 by multiplying by UnitStep[x].

Example:
f[x_] = (1/k) Exp[-x/k] UnitStep[x];
g[x_] = (1/p) Exp[-x/p] UnitStep[x];
Convolve[f[x], g[x], x, y]

This gives the expected convolution up to the step factor. I can’t confirm anything specific about reflecting the Polya-Aeppli distribution beyond that.

