If you mean a true hyperbola in Mathematica, a common form is something like 1/x or a shifted/scaled version. If you share the exact hyperbola equation and what “grey family of lines” means in your plot, I can help adapt this to ParametricPlot and the styling you want.

The material doesn’t directly address “chopping pure Imaginary numbers.” It only shows that Mathematica can be sensitive to tiny numerical values and that rationalizing entries may help stabilize matrix operations.

Closest supported guidance: if your result contains very small numeric leftovers, try converting them with Rationalize before further matrix work. For example:

c = Rationalize[Cttt, 10^(-50)];
e = Rationalize[E0, 10^(-50)];

If you meant removing tiny imaginary roundoff terms, the evidence doesn’t explicitly cover that, but it does support cleaning numerical noise before continuing.

If you want the image of a complex-function map over a specified domain, use a complex region and map it with ComplexMap, making sure the source domain covers a full 2π interval in the imaginary direction. For example, a strip like -π <= Im[z] <= π is supported.

A supported pattern is:

strip = ComplexRegionDraw[0 <= Re[z] <= 1, {z, -2 - Pi I, 2 + Pi I}];
image = Draw2D[{Opacity[0.6], strip // ComplexMap[Exp]},
  Frame -> True, Axes -> True, PlotRange -> 4, FrameLabel -> {Re, Im}, PlotLabel -> w];

This will show the mapped region rather than a blank plot. It doesn’t specifically add stippling or dot shading, but it is the closest supported method shown here.

