If you mean a circular arc or a part of a circular image, the closest supported approach is to rotate a fixed element around the center rather than trying to compute a slope directly. For example, in Mathematica:

Manipulate[
 Graphics[{Circle[], Rotate[Arrow[{{1, 0}, {1, 1}}], φ, {0, 0}]}],
 {φ, 0, 2 Pi}
]

This places the arrow tangent to the circle at the chosen angle. If your “part of an image” is something else, I can only confirm this rotation-based method from the available guidance.

I can’t answer how to plot and animate a hyperbole from the provided information. The only supported guidance is that if you want to visualize small differences between curves, you can plot them together and use conditional filling to highlight the gap:

Plot[{curve1, curve2}, {H, 0, 700},
 PlotStyle -> {Directive[Black, Thick], Black},
 Filling -> {1 -> {{2}, {Yellow, Transparent}}}
]

If your hyperbole is part of a family of curves, the same general approach could be adapted by plotting the set together and varying shading or line thickness by parameter.

Use Solve, then extract the replacement rule you want and, if needed, put it in a list. For example:

sol = Solve[Eq4 == 0, k1];
k1expr = k1 /. First[sol];

If you want a list containing the result, wrap it in braces:

{k1expr}

If Solve returns multiple solutions, choose one with First, Last, or [[1]] before applying /.

If you want your function to behave more like Plot, the supported approach is to break it into separate plots over the ranges you want to keep, then combine them with Show.

For example:

a = Plot[f[x], {x, x1, x2}];
b = Plot[g[x], {x, x2, x3}];
Show[a, b, PlotRange -> All]

This is the way to exclude intervals while still displaying a single graph. If you need to skip more ranges, make one plot per allowed interval and combine them with Show.
